[stderr] ---
[stderr] task_id: T001
[stderr] sequence: 1
[stderr] title: Describe one small, reviewable change
[stderr] browser_impact: none
[stderr] playwright_spec:
[stderr] ---
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Objective
[stderr] 
[stderr] Describe the smallest complete outcome. State what is in scope and, equally
[stderr] important, what must not be changed.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Acceptance criteria
[stderr] 
[stderr] - The intended behaviour is implemented and documented where it affects users or developers.
[stderr] - Focused automated tests cover the change and pass.
[stderr] - No unrelated refactors, network calls, or credentials are introduced.
[stderr] ---
[stderr] task_id: F014
[stderr] sequence: 14
[stderr] title: Generate the bounded implementation-runbook authoring collection
[stderr] browser_impact: not_applicable
[stderr] playwright_spec:
[stderr] ---
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Objective
[stderr] 
[stderr] Generate only the bootstrap wave of ordinary Supervisor runbooks in
[stderr] `projects/<project-slug>/authoring-runbooks/`: a bounded catalogue-expansion
[stderr] task followed by a dispatcher task. The dispatcher allocates at most ten
[stderr] authoring B-series tasks per wave, creates one successor dispatcher, and stops.
[stderr] Each authoring task instructs Codex to create only its assigned R-series
[stderr] implementation runbooks under
[stderr] `projects/<project-slug>/runbooks/`, then validate their metadata and update the
[stderr] manifest. The successor dispatcher repeats this process until no queued
[stderr] catalogue chapter or unassigned implementation contract remains. Generate an
[stderr] `INITIAL.md` context document in `authoring-runbooks/`
[stderr] that points to the canonical `PROJECT_BRIEF.md` and specification.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Register the generated authoring collection with the source collection by
[stderr] writing `runbooks/.supervisor-children/<project-slug>.json` containing
[stderr] `{"runbooks_dir": "../projects/<project-slug>/authoring-runbooks"}`. Also
[stderr] write `authoring-runbooks/.supervisor-children/implementation.json` containing
[stderr] `{"runbooks_dir": "../runbooks"}`. These are explicit child-collection
[stderr] registrations; `supervisor-run --run-all` follows them automatically after the
[stderr] parent collection is accepted.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every dispatcher first expands at most one queued specification/catalogue domain
[stderr] chapter (maximum 250 `IMP-*` contracts), validates its coverage, then allocates
[stderr] ready contracts. If any queued chapter or ready contract remains, it creates no
[stderr] more than ten authoring tasks and exactly one successor dispatcher. If none
[stderr] remains, it creates the final audit task instead. This is a durable loop, not a
[stderr] recursive prompt or an unbounded single run.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every generated R-series runbook must use the standard Supervisor front matter
[stderr] and contain: precise objective; dependencies; relevant requirement IDs; owned
[stderr] files/data/interfaces; required behaviour; non-goals; platform differences;
[stderr] verification commands/evidence; and observable acceptance criteria. It must be
[stderr] small enough for one implementation task, not a vague epic.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every generated R-series runbook must retain machine-readable provenance to the
[stderr] canonical decisions that justify it. Its front matter must declare all of these
[stderr] non-empty fields, using comma-separated values where more than one applies:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```yaml
[stderr] source_specifications: specification/03-technical-contract.md#save-state
[stderr] source_catalogue_ids: IMP-SAVE-001
[stderr] authoring_batch: B0007
[stderr] factory_stages: F003,F005,F012,F013
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] `source_specifications` points to the smallest relevant canonical
[stderr] specification section; `source_catalogue_ids` identifies the allocated
[stderr] implementation-catalogue records; `authoring_batch` identifies the B-series
[stderr] writer that created the R file; and `factory_stages` identifies the F-series
[stderr] stages that established the requirement. This is a traceability trail, not an
[stderr] instruction to inject every F or B runbook into the implementation agent's
[stderr] prompt. The R task uses these links to read only the canonical context it needs.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every generated R-series runbook must also include a detailed asset assessment.
[stderr] Its front matter must always declare both fields below, even when no assets are
[stderr] needed:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```yaml
[stderr] asset_impact: required # or not_applicable
[stderr] asset_ids: village_gate_main,village_gate_construction # blank only when not_applicable
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] For `asset_impact: required`, the B-series writer must reserve stable,
[stderr] product-specific asset IDs (never generic names such as `image1`), provide an
[stderr] `asset_brief` and `visual_style_version`, and include an `## Asset assessment`
[stderr] section explaining the assets' purpose, where they appear, the required visual
[stderr] states/variants, source-of-truth ownership, and original-art acceptance checks.
[stderr] For `not_applicable`, that section must explain why the scope needs no generated
[stderr] or source visual asset and explicitly prohibit incidental asset creation. This
[stderr] keeps UI, illustration, icon, texture, sprite, audio-visual, and other product
[stderr] asset work visible in the implementation plan instead of discovering it midway
[stderr] through coding.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every generated R-series runbook must also declare an audio assessment, even
[stderr] when audio is not needed:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```yaml
[stderr] audio_impact: required # or not_applicable
[stderr] audio_ids: ember_menu_loop # blank only when not_applicable
[stderr] audio_brief: 30-second seamless instrumental menu cue # blank only when not_applicable
[stderr] audio_duration_seconds: 30 # 0 only when not_applicable
[stderr] audio_loop: required # required, not_required, or not_applicable
[stderr] audio_style_version: project-audio-v1
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] For `audio_impact: required`, the writer must reserve stable cue IDs, cite the
[stderr] relevant `04-audio-direction.md` section, describe the in-product trigger and
[stderr] mix/accessibility expectations, and assign a sensible duration and loop policy.
[stderr] The Supervisor's separate local audio lane then creates a manifest, invokes
[stderr] ACE-Step, and performs technical/provenance QA before coding begins. For
[stderr] `not_applicable`, every audio field has the stated empty/default form so an
[stderr] otherwise unrelated task cannot silently generate music or sound effects.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Each B-series authoring task may create at most seven R-series implementation
[stderr] runbooks. It must stop after its assigned seven (or fewer), update the manifest,
[stderr] and leave remaining work for the next bounded batch.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Acceptance criteria
[stderr] 
[stderr] - The initial collection contains only a bounded bootstrap wave; each
[stderr]   dispatcher expands at most one 250-contract domain shard, creates at most ten
[stderr]   new B-series authoring tasks plus one successor, then exits. It never attempts
[stderr]   to materialise the whole game plan.
[stderr] - There is exactly one valid B-series authoring runbook per allocated manifest
[stderr]   batch, and it creates no more than seven assigned R-series runbooks.
[stderr] - B-series runbooks never overwrite an existing R-series contract with a
[stderr]   conflicting ID; they stop and report mismatches.
[stderr] - Every generated B-series runbook includes its context packet, output list,
[stderr]   validation procedure, checkpoint/update procedure, and a clear stop condition.
[stderr] - Both child-collection registration files exist, contain valid JSON with a
[stderr]   relative `runbooks_dir`, and point to the generated authoring and
[stderr]   implementation collections respectively.
[stderr] - R-series contracts use stable IDs and parse with the Supervisor runbook
[stderr]   loader; their declared dependencies match the manifest, every contract has
[stderr]   explicit `asset_impact` and `asset_ids` metadata, and asset-required
[stderr]   contracts have non-empty stable IDs. Every R contract also has non-empty,
[stderr]   valid `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, `authoring_batch`,
[stderr]   and `factory_stages` provenance metadata.
[stderr] - Every R-series contract has explicit valid audio metadata. Audio-required
[stderr]   contracts have stable IDs, a non-empty cue brief, duration, loop policy,
[stderr]   audio-style version, and a source link to the canonical audio direction.
[stderr] - The collection contains a final audit B-series task after all authoring
[stderr]   batches and catalogue expansions are complete, rather than pretending that
[stderr]   thousands of contracts can be reviewed in one generation step.
[stderr] # 03 — Technical, data, trust, and quality contract
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Inputs:** [`INITIAL.md`](../INITIAL.md), [product brief](01-product-brief.md),
[stderr] and [feature model](02-feature-model.md). This contract makes the P0 offline
[stderr] single-player release implementable without selecting a vendor or creating a
[stderr] dependency on the contradictory connected-service requests. It is a design
[stderr] contract, not permission to collect data or operate services.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Architecture and requirement traceability
[stderr] 
[stderr] The production architecture is a static, client-side 2D web game: a shared
[stderr] deterministic domain core, browser presentation/input adapters, versioned
[stderr] authored content, and local durable storage. The web host may deliver a signed
[stderr] release bundle and service worker, but it is not a campaign authority. The
[stderr] core must have no browser APIs, timers, network calls, or renderer dependencies.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Component / boundary | Requirement served | Contract |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Game shell and responsive renderer | J1, J3, J6; readable mobile-to-desktop play | Owns navigation, focus/visibility pause, semantic UI, visual settings, and rendering only. It requests domain commands and renders returned state/events. |
[stderr] | Input adapters | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad accessibility | Convert platform events into a shared, remappable `InputAction` vocabulary; unavailable hardware is non-fatal. No adapter may mutate campaign state directly. |
[stderr] | Deterministic rules core | Tutorial combat, quests, inventory, progression | Owns legal state transitions, seeded/randomness policy, combat resolution, reward ledger, and invariants. Command validation precedes every mutation. |
[stderr] | Content registry and validator | One town/dungeon, original content, localisation | Loads the active versioned content package; resolves IDs/keys and rejects invalid packages before play. It is read-only at runtime. |
[stderr] | Save/recovery repository | J5, long-session recovery | Owns atomic local snapshot writes, validation, quarantine, migration, retention, export/import when approved, and explicit deletion. |
[stderr] | PWA release manager | J7, install/offline/update safety | Owns precache manifest, cache integrity, install affordance, and safe-boundary activation. It never replaces a running release or a pending save write. |
[stderr] | Error boundary and consent-gated diagnostics | Crash/error recovery and privacy | Shows retry/title/recovery actions. Local diagnostic records are minimal and removable; no telemetry transport exists in P0. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Dependency direction and state rules
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] authored content ──> content validator ──> deterministic rules core <── input actions
[stderr]                                             │
[stderr] web/PWA shell + renderer <── state/events ─┼──> save/recovery repository
[stderr]         │                                   │
[stderr]         └──────────> PWA release manager ──┴──> error boundary / local diagnostics
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] Only the rules core can produce durable campaign state. The shell owns
[stderr] ephemeral UI state (open panels, selection, animation), while the repository
[stderr] owns only serialized snapshots and recovery metadata. Rendering, input,
[stderr] caching, and diagnostics must not be required to make a legal game decision.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Data contract
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Ownership, lifecycle, and retention
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Data class | Owner and lifecycle | Persistence / retention | Access and recovery |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Content package | Build/content owner: authored → schema-validated → bundled → immutable release artifact → superseded | Public static assets; current and previous known-good cache retained per cache policy | Read by client only; invalid references reject package before gameplay. |
[stderr] | Campaign snapshot | Player; new game → checkpoint/manual save → valid, migrated, quarantined, or explicitly deleted | Browser-origin local storage appropriate for structured durable data (implementation selects IndexedDB or equivalent); retain current valid slot plus last-known-good checkpoint | Same browser origin only in P0; player may load, delete after confirmation, and receive export/support guidance where feasible. |
[stderr] | Settings | Player; default → previewed → applied → persisted/reset | Separate local preference record; current session remains active if persistence fails | Read/write only by local game; reset requires confirmation. |
[stderr] | Recovery metadata | Client; write attempt/failure → recovery offer → resolved | Local, bounded and tied to save slot; remove with slot/delete-local-data action | Never overwrites valid snapshot; aids quarantine and fallback. |
[stderr] | Diagnostic record | Player-controlled, consent-gated; fault → local record → optional future export/delete | Bounded local record, no identifiers, no campaign dialogue, and no network sending in P0 | Player can decline, clear, or export only after a future approved workflow. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] The local campaign is the P0 source of truth. There is no account, remote
[stderr] profile, analytics stream, purchase ledger, match state, or cloud backup.
[stderr] Remote account-linked replication is a later optional mirror—not campaign truth
[stderr] until approval defines identity, vendor, consent, cost, retention, deletion,
[stderr] support, conflict UX, and outage behaviour.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Versioned content and save schemas
[stderr] 
[stderr] All high-volume authored data uses a versioned, machine-validated package:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] ContentPackage { packageId, semanticVersion, schemaVersion, localeDefault,
[stderr]   scenes[], exits[], dialogues[], quests[], encounters[], enemyDefinitions[],
[stderr]   itemDefinitions[], rewards[], localisation[], assetManifest[] }
[stderr] SaveSnapshot { schemaVersion, contentPackageId, contentVersion, slotId,
[stderr]   revision, checkpointId, campaign, quests, party, inventory, rewardLedger,
[stderr]   settingsReference?, checksum, committedAt }
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] - **Ownership and authoring:** designated game/content owners maintain source
[stderr]   data in repository-controlled files and original asset references. The exact
[stderr]   editor/format is an implementation choice; generated bundles are derived,
[stderr]   never hand-edited.
[stderr] - **Validation:** CI validates JSON/schema shape, unique stable IDs, required
[stderr]   fields, asset and localisation-key existence, valid quest predicates,
[stderr]   scene/spawn/exit targets, legal reward/item/enemy references, no duplicate
[stderr]   reward IDs, and reachability from opening scene to ending. It also rejects
[stderr]   missing required translations in any release locale and unsafe quest-item
[stderr]   removal paths.
[stderr] - **Relationship rules:** every required quest path has a reachable transition;
[stderr]   each locked exit predicate refers to an existing quest state; each encounter
[stderr]   has legal targets/actions and a completion path; all rewards resolve to
[stderr]   extant definitions. Tests include valid minimal package, each broken
[stderr]   reference class, and a graph traversal proving the authored campaign is
[stderr]   completable.
[stderr] - **Import/export:** P0 imports content only at build time through the
[stderr]   validator and packages it into the release. Player save export/import is not
[stderr]   promised; if added, it must use a versioned envelope, parse/validate before
[stderr]   write, never replace a valid save until confirmation, and reject untrusted
[stderr]   payloads safely.
[stderr] - **Compatibility:** each release declares supported content and save schema
[stderr]   ranges. Incompatible saves are quarantined with their original bytes and a
[stderr]   clear fallback/new-game action; a migration must create a new candidate and
[stderr]   validate it before promotion.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Commands, validation, idempotency, and concurrency
[stderr] 
[stderr] The core exposes typed intent commands such as `StartCampaign`,
[stderr] `ChooseAction`, `UseItem`, `Talk`, `AcceptQuest`, `ClaimReward`,
[stderr] `EnterExit`, `CommitCheckpoint`, `LoadSlot`, and `ApplySettings`. A command
[stderr] contains an expected campaign revision and an interaction/action ID. The core
[stderr] returns either a typed rejection without mutation or an ordered state/event
[stderr] result with a new revision.
[stderr] 
[stderr] - Validate session state, command shape, actor/target existence, availability,
[stderr]   eligibility, quest predicate, and expected revision before mutation. UI
[stderr]   selections are untrusted/stale inputs even though all execution is local.
[stderr] - Use stable `rewardId`/`grantId` ledger entries and interaction IDs. Retrying
[stderr]   an acknowledged reward, quest completion, or checkpoint returns the prior
[stderr]   result rather than granting twice.
[stderr] - One command is processed at a time per active campaign. Disable/debounce
[stderr]   duplicate controls while pending; compare expected revision on completion.
[stderr]   Visibility changes, gamepad repeats, and multiple tabs cannot interleave
[stderr]   transitions. A second tab must be warned/read-only or acquire an explicit
[stderr]   local slot lease; stale writers fail without replacing the latest revision.
[stderr] - Combat resolution uses an explicit deterministic seed/state recorded at
[stderr]   safe boundaries. It never persists an unresolved turn; a crash restores the
[stderr]   prior checkpoint, avoiding half-consumed items, half-applied damage, or
[stderr]   duplicate rewards.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Persistence, migration, and failure semantics
[stderr] 
[stderr] Save commit is a write-ahead replacement protocol: serialize a complete
[stderr] snapshot → validate checksum/schema/invariants → write candidate under a new
[stderr] revision → verify read-back → mark current → retain prior known-good snapshot.
[stderr] Implementations must use browser storage semantics that make the final pointer
[stderr] switch atomic, or emulate it with immutable revision records and a validated
[stderr] current pointer. Never clear the prior save before the new pointer is valid.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Condition | Required behaviour |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Storage quota, permission, or unavailable local storage | Keep session playable; show non-blocking save failure and retry guidance; do not claim persistence succeeded. |
[stderr] | Interrupted/crashed write | Ignore incomplete candidate, retain/reload prior confirmed snapshot, and show recovery state at next launch. |
[stderr] | Malformed/checksum-invalid save | Quarantine bytes and metadata; do not overwrite; offer last valid checkpoint/new game and support/export guidance where feasible. |
[stderr] | Migration failure or unsupported version | Preserve original, fail closed into quarantine, and offer clear version/fallback choices. |
[stderr] | Bad content package/reference | Fail build/CI; if discovered at runtime, stop the affected route, retain save, and offer title/retry rather than corrupting campaign state. |
[stderr] | Cache/network refresh failure | Continue with known-good cached release; explain first-install offline limitation without pretending an uncached release is playable. |
[stderr] | Application fault/crash loop | Error boundary offers retry, title, and safe recovery; diagnostics remain local unless explicitly consented and a future transport is approved. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Migration functions are ordered, pure transformations from one explicit schema
[stderr] version to the next, with fixtures for every supported predecessor. They must
[stderr] be idempotent or record the source version so a retry cannot apply a migration
[stderr] twice. A release may remove old migrations only after a separately approved
[stderr] support/retention decision.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Trust, privacy, and security
[stderr] 
[stderr] - Treat content, imported saves, URL parameters, browser events, service-worker
[stderr]   messages, and persisted bytes as untrusted. Validate and bound sizes before
[stderr]   parsing; never execute content as code or render dialogue via unsafe HTML.
[stderr] - Serve production assets over HTTPS with a restrictive content security policy,
[stderr]   no third-party scripts by default, dependency lock/review, and build-time
[stderr]   asset integrity/hash manifest. Do not embed credentials in the client.
[stderr] - Use same-origin storage isolation; minimise persisted data to game state and
[stderr]   chosen preferences. Do not collect names, contact data, precise location,
[stderr]   advertising IDs, account identifiers, payment data, or gameplay analytics in
[stderr]   P0.
[stderr] - Diagnostics default off where consent is required and must state fields,
[stderr]   purpose, retention, deletion, and transport before collection. A local
[stderr]   diagnostic record excludes free-text dialogue and complete save contents.
[stderr] - There is no authorization role in P0 because there is no server resource or
[stderr]   identity boundary. Locally, a player controls their browser profile; this is
[stderr]   not a security guarantee against a device owner. Any server feature requires
[stderr]   explicit least-privilege roles, authenticated authorization on every request,
[stderr]   rate limits, audit events, deletion/retention policy, incident ownership, and
[stderr]   documented offline/outage behaviour before implementation.
[stderr] - No copied branding, assets, text, layouts, or distinctive interaction
[stderr]   sequences enter the content pipeline. Provenance/review is required for
[stderr]   shipped art, audio, writing, and dependencies.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Target-platform matrix
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Contract area | Responsive public web application | Installable PWA |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Shared code/data boundary | Same rules core, content package, save schema, semantic actions, accessibility settings, and release compatibility rules. Browser adapter owns viewport/input differences. | Same shared core/data. Service worker, installation, cache lifecycle, and platform install affordance are PWA adapters. |
[stderr] | Input and device capability | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where exposed; feature-detect and show available mappings. Handle resize, rotation, focus/visibility, reduced-motion/contrast settings where available. | Same inputs and feature detection; no assumed hardware merely because installed. Installation, notifications, or storage-persistence prompts are optional and must not block play. |
[stderr] | Offline and recovery | May play offline only after relevant assets are cached. First uncached visit tells player an initial download/network is required. Local save protocol applies. | Primary offline route after successful install/cache. Precaches approved shell/content, retains known-good cache on refresh failure, and keeps saves independent of cache eviction. |
[stderr] | Build and test | Produce hashed static assets, manifest, and browser-compatible bundle. Test core/unit/content validation, responsive layouts, input adaptations, save corruption/migration, error states, and supported browser/device/network matrix. | Build includes service worker and install manifest. Test installability, offline reload, cache rollback/update-at-safe-boundary, storage pressure, and all shared checks. |
[stderr] | Accessibility and performance | Keyboard-operable menus, semantic labels, visible focus, readable scalable text, captions, non-colour cues, settings persistence, responsive phone/tablet/desktop checks. Establish approval-gated performance budgets for initial load, interaction frame time, memory, bundle/cache size, and slow-network behaviour before release. | Same accessibility/performance requirements, plus offline messaging, update state, install prompt alternatives, and no inaccessible full-screen/install-only path. Measure cold/warm/offline startup and cache size. |
[stderr] | Release channel and policy | Immutable versioned static release to approved web hosting; staged/canary and rollback process must be selected before launch. Publish supported-browser matrix, privacy notice if diagnostics changes, and recovery notes. | Browser-mediated install/update channel; manifest/icon/display policy and service-worker behaviour must meet supported browser/platform rules. Prompt only at safe boundaries; release notes and rollback retain prior runnable cache. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Support targets are expressed as capability tests rather than invented minimum
[stderr] OS versions: current and previous major releases of agreed evergreen browsers
[stderr] on representative phone, tablet, and desktop classes; exact versions/devices,
[stderr] browser engine exclusions, storage quota, and slow-network thresholds remain a
[stderr] release approval gate.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Observability, quality evidence, and release verification
[stderr] 
[stderr] P0 observability is local and player-respecting: structured console/build logs
[stderr] in development, release/version/cache status visible in support UI, and an
[stderr] optional bounded local fault record. It has no remote monitoring dependency.
[stderr] Every fault has a player-facing recovery path and a test that asserts retained
[stderr] save/cache behaviour as applicable.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Evidence layer | Required checks |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Rules and data | Unit/property tests for legal/illegal commands, revision rejection, idempotent grants, deterministic combat fixtures, save encode/decode/checksum, migration fixtures, recovery/quarantine, and content schema/reachability/reference validation. |
[stderr] | Integration | Test shell-to-core action mapping, checkpoint ordering, focus/pause, unavailable input fallback, settings persistence failure, cache/save independence, and PWA safe update behaviour. |
[stderr] | Target/browser | Run agreed browser/device matrix for keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad-adapted paths, responsive viewport checks, offline first/repeat visit, install flow where supported, storage failure, and browser accessibility audit/manual assistive-technology checks. |
[stderr] | Performance/security | Capture approved load/frame/memory/cache budgets on representative low-capability and slow-network profiles; dependency/license/provenance review, CSP/header check, no-secret scan, and import/parser fuzz/bounds tests. |
[stderr] | Release gate | Versioned content/save compatibility declaration, clean content validation, migration/recovery suite, PWA offline/update/rollback check, accessibility and performance results, privacy/diagnostic confirmation, and tested rollback to a known-good release. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Release is blocked when validation cannot prove campaign reachability, a save
[stderr] could overwrite the last known-good state, an update can activate mid-combat or
[stderr] mid-write, an unsupported capability blocks the core path without explanation,
[stderr] or any mandatory quality budget/matrix remains unapproved or unmeasured.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Decisions deferred for approval
[stderr] 
[stderr] These are intentionally not selected by this contract: game engine/framework,
[stderr] rendering library, static host/CDN, CI provider, diagnostics vendor/transport,
[stderr] exact storage API/quota, browser/OS/device minimums, performance thresholds,
[stderr] languages, age/parental/regional obligations, service-worker rollout policy,
[stderr] and content editor format. Account identity, remote save replication,
[stderr] multiplayer/player safety, live operations, analytics, purchases, and
[stderr] entitlements require separate product, security, privacy, cost, failure-mode,
[stderr] # 04 — Experience and accessibility contract
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Inputs:** [product brief](01-product-brief.md), [feature model](02-feature-model.md), and [technical, data, trust, and quality contract](03-technical-contract.md). This is the experience contract for the P0 offline campaign: one town, one dungeon, turn-based combat, inventory, quests, saves, settings, and a short ending. It specifies behaviour and presentation without choosing an engine, a library, exact art assets, or an unapproved connected service.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Experience premise and originality boundary
[stderr] 
[stderr] The player arrives as a wayfinder in **Larkspur Reach**, a small hillside town whose old beacon has gone dark. A local request leads through the nearby **Glassroot Hollow** to relight it. This is a compact, hopeful mystery about attention, repair, and returning safely—not a borrowed world, cast, plot, place, or visual identity. The ending is a quiet return to the town with the beacon restored and the campaign marked complete.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The visual language is original: warm hand-painted fantasy with broad, readable silhouettes; worn clay, moss, lantern-gold, plum shadow, and rain-blue accents; gently textured scenery; and simple, high-contrast interface planes. Characters, creature designs, maps, names, dialogue, quest structure, UI composition, animation timing, sound, and gameplay choreography must be independently authored. The functional reference informs only the broad capability of a party-based, turn-based adventure. It must not inform trade dress, screen layouts, menus, copy, world-building, assets, or distinctive sequences.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Information architecture and navigation
[stderr] 
[stderr] The shell has a persistent, semantic navigation model. The game view is the primary workspace; panels are temporary layers rather than separate unexplained destinations.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] Launch / title
[stderr] ├── New game → accessibility quick setup (skippable) → tutorial → town → dungeon → ending
[stderr] ├── Continue / Load → slot picker → restored safe boundary → game view
[stderr] ├── Settings → accessibility, controls, audio, display, language (when supplied)
[stderr] ├── Help & controls → input-specific action reference
[stderr] ├── Save data → load, delete-confirmation, recovery status
[stderr] ├── Privacy & diagnostics → local-data explanation, consent / clear actions
[stderr] └── PWA status → install guidance, offline readiness, safe update state
[stderr] 
[stderr] Game view
[stderr] ├── objective / quest panel
[stderr] ├── inventory and party panels
[stderr] ├── pause menu → save, settings, help, return to title
[stderr] └── contextual interaction / dialogue / combat layers
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] - The title exposes only available actions. With no valid save, `Continue` and `Load` are absent or disabled with the accompanying explanation; `New game` remains the primary action.
[stderr] - `Escape`, a visible pause affordance, and the mapped gamepad pause action open pause at any safe playable state. Focus loss or tab hiding pauses the game and explains this only once per session.
[stderr] - Every panel has a visible title, close control, and a known return target. Closing restores the prior selection and scroll position where feasible. Browser Back never silently discards progress: it closes a transient panel first; leaving the game prompts only when an unsaved, recoverable change would be lost.
[stderr] - Modal layers are reserved for blocking choices: save deletion, settings reset, a new-game replacement of an existing active slot, and a recovery choice. They state consequence, affected slot/data, irreversible status, and a safe default (`Cancel`). No timed dialog expires.
[stderr] - Navigation labels use player language: `Quest`, `Bag`, `Party`, `Save`, `Settings`, and `Help`. Icons always have adjacent text or an accessible name; an icon alone is never the only cue.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Story, content, and feedback principles
[stderr] 
[stderr] Writing is concise, warm, and grounded. Dialogue advances in short, skimmable turns: speaker name, one thought or action, then a clear response/continue choice. Quest copy answers **what happened**, **what to do next**, and **where to go** without hiding required direction in decorative prose. The tutorial teaches one action at a time: move/interact, select a combat action and target, observe the result, then save. It never assumes prior genre knowledge.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Narrative presentation gives players control: advance, review the current exchange, and enable captions; it does not auto-advance essential text. A quest update is both spoken/written in the panel and visibly confirmed near the objective. Victory, gained item, blocked route, invalid action, save completion, update-ready, and recovery messages use a combination of short text, an icon or shape, optional audio/haptic feedback where the platform supports it, and a non-blocking visual change. Colour and sound are supplementary, never the sole signal.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Combat is an ordered decision loop: announce whose turn it is, offer legal actions, show required target selection, then resolve with an interruptible/reduced-motion-friendly result and return to the next decision. Illegal or unavailable choices explain why and preserve the current turn. Exploration interactions use an explicit prompt with the action name; locked routes name the missing condition rather than merely refusing movement.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Layout, visual tokens, and responsive behaviour
[stderr] 
[stderr] The game scene retains its 2D world view while the interface reflows around it. Essential information is never permanently covered by a decorative frame, cut off by device notches, or dependent on hover.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Token / rule | Contract |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `--surface-base`, `--surface-raised` | Warm parchment/dark-plum interface planes. Body text and essential icons meet at least **4.5:1** contrast; large text (at least 24 CSS px regular or 18.66 CSS px bold) meets **3:1**. |
[stderr] | `--text-primary`, `--text-muted` | Primary is used for all essential content. Muted text is never the only carrier of an instruction, state, or warning and meets 4.5:1 when conveying necessary text. |
[stderr] | `--accent-action`, `--state-success/warning/danger` | Accent is paired with text and a distinct icon/shape. Focus does not rely on the action colour. Error and destructive states use wording plus iconography. |
[stderr] | `--focus-ring` | A high-visibility 3 CSS-px minimum outline with at least 3:1 contrast against adjacent colours; never removed. It has clear offset from the focused control. |
[stderr] | Typography | Sans-serif UI type with clear letterforms; text scaling is supported to 200% without clipping, overlap, horizontal page scrolling, or lost actions. Body/default dialogue begins at a readable base size; users may choose larger text and line spacing. |
[stderr] | Spacing and targets | Minimum 44 by 44 CSS px target for touch-operable controls, with 8 CSS px separation where adjacent targets could be confused. Targets are not made smaller on compact screens. |
[stderr] | Motion | Motion is brief, non-essential, and can be reduced. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; provide an in-game reduction setting that removes camera shake, parallax, flashes, auto-panning, and nonessential transitions. No essential timing depends on animation. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Viewport | Layout behaviour |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Phone / narrow portrait | Full-width world view with safe-area insets. Objective is a compact expandable summary; action controls form a bottom reachable sheet. Inventory, quest, settings, and dialogue become full-screen panels with a sticky close/back control. Combat actions stack or use a single-column selection list; no critical control is below the browser chrome. |
[stderr] | Tablet / medium | World stays primary with a side or bottom contextual panel. Combat has a clear action area and target area side-by-side when space allows; otherwise it uses the phone sequence. Panels never overlap the action prompt. |
[stderr] | Desktop / wide | Centred game stage with persistent but collapsible objective/status rail. Inventory and quest can use a two-column inspection layout; combat keeps actions and party/enemy status simultaneously visible. Mouse hover may preview a definition but never supplies essential information. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Rotation, resize, zoom, virtual keyboards, and PWA display-mode changes preserve the current game state and selection where practical. If reflow temporarily obscures the selected control, focus moves to the panel heading and a short status message announces the layout change. Full screen is optional and provides an obvious exit path.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Input and interaction adaptation
[stderr] 
[stderr] All supported physical inputs invoke one semantic action vocabulary: move, inspect/interact, confirm, cancel/pause, open quest, open bag, open settings, navigate previous/next, and choose a legal combat action/target. A player can complete every core journey without pointer precision, dragging, multi-touch, rapid repetition, or a time limit.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Surface / input | Required adaptation |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Keyboard | Arrow keys and WASD move/navigate where appropriate; Tab and Shift+Tab move through UI controls in visual reading order; Enter/Space confirm; Escape cancels/closes/pause. Avoid keyboard traps; provide an early `Skip to game controls` link from the shell. Key repeat must not confirm a destructive choice or consume multiple turn actions. Control mappings are visible and remappable subject to the later-approved configuration depth; conflicts are explained before saving. |
[stderr] | Mouse / trackpad | Click targets match the semantic controls; hover is optional enrichment only. Right-click is not required. The pointer never needs to trace tiny map details to perform a required interaction. |
[stderr] | Touch | On-screen controls are reachable, labelled, and may be toggled in settings. Use tap-to-confirm and explicit target selection; do not require pinch, swipe-only navigation, double tap, drag, or long press. A touch action has the same feedback as its keyboard equivalent. |
[stderr] | Gamepad | Feature-detect rather than promise a model. Show button glyphs only after detection and accompany them with action text. D-pad/left stick navigates; primary face button confirms; secondary face button cancels; menu button pauses; shoulder buttons may switch top-level panels when discoverable. A disconnected controller preserves state and presents keyboard/mouse/touch alternatives without blocking. |
[stderr] | Remote / focus-only devices | These are not a selected delivery target. If a browser presents only directional/confirm/back focus input, the menus use the gamepad-style focus order and all core controls remain reachable; continuous exploration is not promised until a target/device decision is approved. |
[stderr] | Voice and other assistive input | Voice command recognition is not collected, enabled, or required in P0. Controls expose standard semantic HTML roles, names, states, and operable buttons so browser voice control, switch control, eye tracking, and other assistive input can target visible actions by label. Canvas/world interactions have equivalent labelled UI prompts and action-list routes. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Accessibility policy
[stderr] 
[stderr] The release target is WCAG 2.2 AA for the public UI and the operable game controls, evaluated with automated checks and manual keyboard, screen-reader, zoom/reflow, touch, and reduced-motion review on the approved browser/device matrix. This is a product quality target; exact assistive-technology combinations remain a release approval gate rather than an invented compatibility claim.
[stderr] 
[stderr] - Use semantic landmarks for banner/title navigation, main game controls, complementary objective/status, and dialogs. Mark decorative art as hidden from assistive technology. Give the world canvas a concise labelled description and provide its essential current state through adjacent semantic status and action elements.
[stderr] - Every interactive element has a unique accessible name that matches its visible label where one exists. Examples: `Open quest: Relight the Beacon`, `Use Moss Tonic on Rowan`, `Close inventory`, `Save slot 1`, and `Delete slot 1`.
[stderr] - Dialogues announce speaker and text through a controlled, non-interrupting status mechanism; combat announces turn start, selected action/target, damage/healing, and victory/defeat without flooding announcements for decorative animation frames. Notifications are concise, deduplicated, and reviewable in the status/quest panel.
[stderr] - Focus is visible at all times, enters a dialog at its heading or first safe action, remains contained only while that blocking dialog is open, and returns to its invoking control when closed. A newly opened non-modal panel receives focus on its heading. Error focus moves to an actionable summary, not an arbitrary visual element.
[stderr] - Do not use flicker above three flashes per second. Provide captions/subtitles for all non-text audio that conveys story, instruction, combat state, or warning; identify speaker and relevant sound in concise text. Master audio, music, effects, caption visibility, text size, contrast, and motion settings are reachable before starting a new game and while paused.
[stderr] - Required state has a text equivalent: party health uses values and state wording as well as bars; enemy intent uses a label/icon; map interaction, quest progression, and save status use text. Visual settings include a higher-contrast mode and non-colour state markers.
[stderr] - Users can pause, slow, or disable nonessential movement and screen effects. No essential dialogue, battle selection, confirmation, error message, or recovery choice disappears by timeout.
[stderr] - Settings save independently of campaign saves where storage permits. If persistence fails, keep the in-session choice, state that it will not persist, and preserve a usable default. Reset settings is confirmed and describes the affected preference categories.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Empty, error, recovery, destructive, and privacy-sensitive states
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Situation | Experience requirement |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Empty inventory / no quest / no save | State what is empty, why it may be empty, and the next useful action: e.g., `No items yet—explore the town and finish the first encounter.` No decorative blank panels. |
[stderr] | Invalid action, locked exit, unavailable target | Keep the player’s state and turn intact; name the unmet requirement and return focus to the legal choices. Do not use a generic `Error` as the only explanation. |
[stderr] | Storage failure, corrupt save, or interrupted save | Never claim a save completed until confirmed. Retain the last valid checkpoint; show retry, return-to-title, and fallback/new-game paths. Quarantined saves are explained as preserved but unusable; recovery text avoids blame. |
[stderr] | Offline, install, cache, or update state | Explain whether the current cached version can play, whether an initial download is required, and that an update will wait for a save-safe title/menu boundary. Declining install or update does not block play on a valid cached/current release. |
[stderr] | Fault / crash recovery | Present plain-language retry, return to title, and safe-recovery actions. Preserve confirmed progress. A diagnostic option is optional and clearly separate from recovery. |
[stderr] | Delete a save | Require a confirmation dialog that names the slot, its progress/time where available, and that deletion is irreversible. Initial focus is `Cancel`; `Delete` is visually and semantically distinct and cannot be activated through a stale/repeated input. Only the selected local slot and associated recovery metadata are removed. |
[stderr] | New game over an active slot / reset settings | Explain precisely what will change. New game must use a separate slot or obtain explicit confirmation before replacing a selected slot. Settings reset affects preferences only, not campaign state. |
[stderr] | Diagnostics and local data | P0 sends no telemetry. Before any consent-gated local diagnostic record is created or exported, state purpose, included minimal fields, retention, deletion control, and that it does not affect play. `Clear diagnostic data` confirms the local-only effect. Never show save contents or dialogue in diagnostics UI. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## PWA-specific experience
[stderr] 
[stderr] The web and installed PWA share the campaign, controls, accessibility settings, semantic UI, and local-save behaviour. Installation is an optional convenience: the title/PWA status area explains availability and has a non-modal fallback when the browser offers no install prompt. Offline status is visible but not alarming. An uncached first visit tells the player that the game cannot begin offline until its assets finish downloading; a known-good installed/cache version continues if an update check fails.
[stderr] 
[stderr] An update notice appears only at a safe boundary (title or paused menu after a confirmed save) and says whether reloading is needed. It offers `Update now` and `Later`; it never reloads during combat, a save write, a modal choice, or essential dialogue. Cache refresh messaging does not imply that save data was refreshed, deleted, or synchronised.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Content and interaction review checklist
[stderr] 
[stderr] Before a content or UI change ships, review it against this contract: original provenance; clear next action and empty/error copy; keyboard, touch, mouse, gamepad, and semantic assistive-input operation; visible focus and accurate accessible names; contrast and non-colour cues; 44 CSS-px touch targets; 200% text/reflow; captions and reduced motion; safe save/update/recovery behaviour; destructive confirmation; and consent/minimisation for any privacy-sensitive interface.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Open decisions retained for approval
[stderr] 
[stderr] - Exact content quantities, party size, localisation languages, full control-remapping depth, audio scope, and supported browser/device/assistive-technology matrix.
[stderr] - The legal, age/parental, regional, and certification policies that may change content warnings or accessibility obligations.
[stderr] - Account identity, remote sync, multiplayer, player safety/moderation, live operations, commerce, and analytics. These remain outside the offline P0 experience; no social, payment, reporting, account, or remote-data UI may be designed as committed functionality without approval.
