\"SHARD-02\",\"title\":\"Campaign content and domain logic\",\"depends_on\":[\"SHARD-01\"],\"state\":\"queued\",\"future_wave\":\"wave 2 after GATE-0004\",\"allocation_rule\":\"expand into bounded IMP records before assigning any R IDs\"},\n    {\"chapter\":\"SHARD-03\",\"title\":\"Session, UI, input, and accessibility\",\"depends_on\":[\"SHARD-01\",\"SHARD-02\"],\"state\":\"queued\",\"future_wave\":\"wave 3 after GATE-0002,GATE-0003,GATE-0004\",\"allocation_rule\":\"expand after its predecessor shards; preserve shared UI and web-adapter ownership\"},\n    {\"chapter\":\"SHARD-04\",\"title\":\"PWA delivery, performance, operations, and release evidence\",\"depends_on\":[\"SHARD-01\",\"SHARD-02\",\"SHARD-03\"],\"state\":\"queued\",\"future_wave\":\"wave 4 after GATE-0002,GATE-0005,GATE-0007\",\"allocation_rule\":\"expand only after the session/UI and campaign contracts exist\"},\n    {\"chapter\":\"SHARD-05\",\"title\":\"Connected-scope decision package\",\"depends_on\":[\"SHARD-01\"],\"state\":\"hold\",\"future_wave\":\"only if explicitly authorised after GATE-0006\",\"allocation_rule\":\"no P0 implementation R contracts\"}\n  ],\n  \"context_packet_baseline\": {\n    \"terminology\": [\"B-series = authoring runbook\", \"R-series = generated implementation runbook\", \"IMP-series = catalogue contract\", \"shared core\", \"web adapter\", \"PWA delivery adapter\", \"local save\", \"cache\", \"human gate\"],\n    \"target_constraints\": [\"original offline-capable 2D single-player RPG\", \"responsive web and installable PWA\", \"keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad\", \"no accounts, sync, multiplayer, purchases, or network services in P0\", \"shared core must not import platform/render/network modules\", \"PWA cache and save data remain separate\"],\n    \"required_templates\": [\"runbooks/TEMPLATE.md\", \"runbooks/F014.md provenance and asset-assessment requirements\"],\n    \"authoring_rules\": [\"write only assigned paths\", \"preserve gates and unanswered questions\", \"do not create incidental assets when asset_impact is not_applicable\", \"perform structural, not product-release, validation\"]\n  },\n  \"batches\": [\n    {\"id\":\"B0001\",\"order\":1,\"title\":\"Bootstrap decision-gated foundation\",\"state\":\"blocked_on_gate\",\"parallel_with\":[],\"preceding_decision\":\"GATE-0001 / DEC-0001 framework, package manager, and CI selection\",\"r_contract_ids\":[\"R0001\"],\"context_packet\":{\"specification\":[\"specification/01-product-brief.md#release-definition\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability\",\"specification/platform-appendix.md\"],\"catalogue_records\":[\"IMP-0001\"]},\"checkpoint\":\"validate R0001 metadata and output ownership, record gate disposition, then checkpoint wave\",\"recovery\":\"keep R0001 reserved; after the gate, rerun B0001 only\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"one bounded R runbook\",\"gate is explicit\",\"baseline command and ownership evidence are specified\"]},\n    {\"id\":\"B0002\",\"order\":2,\"title\":\"Shared seams, deterministic harness, and token contract\",\"state\":\"planned\",\"depends_on_batches\":[\"B0001\"],\"parallel_with\":[],\"preceding_decision\":\"B0001 accepted; R0012's visual-token portion remains blocked by GATE-0002,GATE-0004\",\"r_contract_ids\":[\"R0002\",\"R0003\",\"R0010\"],\"context_packet\":{\"specification\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#commands-validation-idempotency-and-concurrency\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#layout-visual-tokens-and-responsive-behaviour\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#accessibility-policy\"],\"catalogue_records\":[\"IMP-0002\",\"IMP-0003\",\"IMP-0010\"]},\"checkpoint\":\"validate the three contracts and record any gate-blocked subwork\",\"recovery\":\"repair only R0002,R0003,R0010; never split or renumber accepted contracts\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"core/adapter seam precedes feature work\",\"fixture and token outputs have distinct ownership\",\"all gates retained\"]},\n    {\"id\":\"B0003\",\"order\":3,\"title\":\"Deterministic state slice and connected-scope exclusion\",\"state\":\"planned\",\"depends_on_batches\":[\"B0002\"],\"parallel_with\":[],\"preceding_decision\":\"B0002 accepted\",\"r_contract_ids\":[\"R0004\",\"R0008\"],\"context_packet\":{\"specification\":[\"specification/02-feature-model.md#domain-vocabulary-and-state-ownership\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#data-contract\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#commands-validation-idempotency-and-concurrency\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security\"],\"catalogue_records\":[\"IMP-0004\",\"IMP-0008\"]},\"checkpoint\":\"validate state/event ownership and negative connected-scope coverage\",\"recovery\":\"retain IDs and repair the failed R contract only\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"vertical foundation slice remains independently reviewable\",\"P0 connected-service exclusion is explicit\"]},\n    {\"id\":\"B0004\",\"order\":4,\"title\":\"Content validation and local recovery protocols\",\"state\":\"planned\",\"depends_on_batches\":[\"B0003\"],\"parallel_with\":[],\"preceding_decision\":\"B0003 accepted; R0006 requires GATE-0003\",\"r_contract_ids\":[\"R0005\",\"R0006\"],\"context_packet\":{\"specification\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#versioned-content-and-save-schemas\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics\",\"specification/02-feature-model.md#save-recovery-and-offline-persistence\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#empty-error-recovery-destructive-and-privacy-sensitive-states\"],\"catalogue_records\":[\"IMP-0005\",\"IMP-0006\"]},\"checkpoint\":\"validate content/save boundaries and retained storage gate\",\"recovery\":\"retain R0005,R0006 and rerun the blocked item after its decision\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"content and save/cache ownership is separated\",\"save recovery remains a bounded foundation slice\"]},\n    {\"id\":\"B0005\",\"order\":5,\"title\":\"Migration, diagnostics, and provenance controls\",\"state\":\"planned\",\"depends_on_batches\":[\"B0004\"],\"parallel_with\":[\"B0006 after B0004 acceptance, except R0007 still waits for R0006\"],\"preceding_decision\":\"R0007 needs GATE-0003; R0009 needs GATE-0005\",\"r_contract_ids\":[\"R0007\",\"R0009\",\"R0011\"],\"context_packet\":{\"specification\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security\",\"specification/01-product-brief.md#originality-boundary\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#experience-premise-and-originality-boundary\"],\"catalogue_records\":[\"IMP-0007\",\"IMP-0009\",\"IMP-0011\"]},\"checkpoint\":\"validate migration, diagnostics, and provenance fields; record unresolved gates\",\"recovery\":\"rerun only incomplete assigned IDs; preserve any accepted R contract\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"no transport or remote diagnostics is introduced\",\"provenance review is explicit\",\"contracts remain at most three\"]},\n    {\"id\":\"B0006\",\"order\":6,\"title\":\"Foundation documentation closure\",\"state\":\"planned\",\"depends_on_batches\":[\"B0004\",\"B0003\"],\"parallel_with\":[\"B0005 after B0004 acceptance\"],\"preceding_decision\":\"R0006 must be accepted or documented as blocked; no gate is implicitly resolved\",\"r_contract_ids\":[\"R0012\"],\"context_packet\":{\"specification\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security\"],\"catalogue_records\":[\"IMP-0012\"]},\"checkpoint\":\"cross-check all foundation ownership, dependencies, and gates before accepting the wave\",\"recovery\":\"retain R0012 and correct only its documentation contract\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"documentation concludes the foundation slice without claiming implementation or approval\",\"gated save work remains visible\"]}\n  ],\n  \"r_contracts\": [\n    {\"id\":\"R0001\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0001\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap reproducible shared project baseline\",\"dependencies\":[],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0001.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/01-product-brief.md#release-definition\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0001\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0001\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No product asset work; establish only project/tooling boundaries and prohibit incidental assets.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"documented deterministic baseline command\",\"isolated ownership directories\",\"GATE-0001 retained\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and path; repair only this contract\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0002\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0002\",\"title\":\"Enforce shared-core and adapter dependency seams\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0001\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0002.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#dependency-direction-and-state-rules\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0002\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0002\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets; this is an interface and dependency-policy contract.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"forbidden core imports rejected\",\"adapter command-only fixture\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and repair only this contract\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0003\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0003\",\"title\":\"Create deterministic test and fixture harness\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0001\",\"R0002\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0003.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#observability-quality-evidence-and-release-verification\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0003\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0002\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets; fixtures use non-product deterministic data.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"repeatable fixture result\",\"isolated fixture storage\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and repair only this contract\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0004\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0004\",\"title\":\"Define deterministic campaign state and command contract\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0002\",\"R0003\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0004.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#data-contract\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#commands-validation-idempotency-and-concurrency\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0004\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0003\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets; state envelopes deliberately exclude authored content.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"stale and invalid commands do not mutate state\",\"duplicate interaction is idempotent\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and repair only this contract\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0005\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0005\",\"title\":\"Define versioned content schema and validation boundary\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0001\",\"R0003\",\"R0004\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0005.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#versioned-content-and-save-schemas\",\"specification/02-feature-model.md#domain-vocabulary-and-state-ownership\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0005\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0004\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No art/audio assets; reserve only schema/provenance fields for later original assets.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"valid minimal package passes\",\"invalid references fail\",\"reachability fixture\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and repair only this contract\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0006\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0006\",\"title\":\"Specify local save and recovery protocol\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0002\",\"R0003\",\"R0004\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0006.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#empty-error-recovery-destructive-and-privacy-sensitive-states\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0006\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0004\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets; protocol is data/recovery only.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"fault fixtures cover write and validation failures\",\"save/cache separation\",\"GATE-0003 retained\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID; wait for storage decision before accepting gated work\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0007\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0007\",\"title\":\"Define pure save migration runner\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0005\",\"R0006\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0007.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0007\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0005\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets; migration fixtures retain original bytes only.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"predecessors migrate once\",\"failed candidate remains intact\",\"GATE-0003 retained\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and resume after R0006/gate closure\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0008\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0008\",\"title\":\"Bound P0 identity, authorization, and connected-service exclusions\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0002\",\"R0004\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0008.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0008\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0003\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets; a negative scope boundary.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"no identity/token/network fields\",\"future connected work remains gated\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and repair only this contract\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0009\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0009\",\"title\":\"Define privacy-safe local diagnostics boundary\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0002\",\"R0003\",\"R0006\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0009.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#empty-error-recovery-destructive-and-privacy-sensitive-states\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0009\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0005\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets; local redacted records only.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"consent and deletion controls\",\"payload redaction\",\"no network transport\",\"GATE-0005 retained\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and wait for privacy gate where required\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0010\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0010\",\"title\":\"Establish semantic accessibility design tokens\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0001\",\"R0002\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0010.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#layout-visual-tokens-and-responsive-behaviour\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#accessibility-policy\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0010\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0002\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No final artwork, fonts, or icons; semantic tokens only and prohibit incidental assets.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"non-colour cues\",\"reduced motion values\",\"GATE-0002,GATE-0004 retained\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and await numeric design decisions\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0011\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0011\",\"title\":\"Create original-content provenance control\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0005\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0011.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/01-product-brief.md#originality-boundary\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#experience-premise-and-originality-boundary\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0011\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0005\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets are created; this contract defines provenance controls for later asset-required work.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"missing provenance rejected\",\"human creative review recorded\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and repair only this contract\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0012\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0012\",\"title\":\"Document foundation architecture and recovery boundaries\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0002\",\"R0004\",\"R0006\",\"R0008\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0012.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0012\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0006\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets; developer-documentation closure only.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"all foundation ownership/dependencies/gates cross-checked\",\"does not claim release readiness\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and repair only this documentation contract\"}\n  ]\n}\n---\ntask_id: R0001\nsequence: 1\ntitle: Bootstrap reproducible shared project baseline\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\nrequirement_ids: [NFR-0010, NFR-0012]\nsource_specifications: [specification/01-product-brief.md#release-definition, specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability]\nsource_catalogue_ids: [IMP-0001]\nauthoring_batch: B0001\nfactory_stages: [F011, F012, F013]\nasset_impact: not_applicable\nasset_ids: []\nasset_brief: No product asset work; establish only project/tooling boundaries and prohibit incidental assets.\nvisual_style_version:\naudio_impact: not_applicable\naudio_ids: []\naudio_brief:\naudio_duration_seconds: 0\naudio_loop: not_applicable\naudio_style_version:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nAfter GATE-0001 / DEC-0001 selects the framework, package manager, and CI,\ncreate the approved-runtime project skeleton and its reproducible shared\nbaseline. Document one bootstrap, lint, format, and deterministic sample-test\ncommand without implementing game, content, rendering, service-worker, or\nhosting behaviour.\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- No R-series dependencies.\n- Implementation is blocked until GATE-0001 / DEC-0001 has approved the\n  framework, package manager, and CI selection.\n\n## Owned files, data, and interfaces\n\n- Root build and tooling configuration for the approved stack.\n- `src/core/`, `src/content/`, `src/app/`, `src/platform/web/`, and\n  `src/platform/pwa/` directory boundaries only.\n- `tests/` directory boundary and one deterministic non-product sample test.\n- `docs/development.md` with the approved toolchain pin and command interface.\n- The pinned toolchain declaration and documented bootstrap/lint/format/test\n  command interface.\n\n## Required behaviour\n\n- Use only the approved GATE-0001 stack choices; record the exact pinned\n  toolchain and the commands required for a clean checkout.\n- Make bootstrap install project-local dependencies reproducibly, then make\n  lint, format checking, and the deterministic sample test separately\n  invocable through documented commands.\n- Establish the listed shared, adapter, content, application, and test\n  directory boundaries without adding domain implementation.\n- Keep the shared core independent of browser APIs, timers, network calls, and\n  renderer dependencies; later contracts own concrete dependency enforcement\n  and adapters.\n- Preserve the web/PWA shared-core rule: adapters may differ by platform but\n  must not create target-specific campaign rules.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Selecting or substituting a framework, package manager, CI provider, host,\n  renderer, storage API, or vendor.\n- Gameplay, campaign state, authored content, UI screens, accessibility token\n  values, persistence, service worker, PWA installation, hosting, or release\n  work.\n- Browser/device compatibility, performance, offline, or release claims.\n- Accounts, network clients, diagnostics transport, credentials, and connected\n  services.\n\n## Platform differences\n\n- The responsive web and PWA targets share this baseline and the same future\n  core/content contracts.\n- Reserve `src/platform/web/` for browser adaptation and `src/platform/pwa/`\n  for later installation/cache adaptation. Do not add a service worker or PWA\n  lifecycle behaviour in this task.\n\n## Asset assessment\n\nNo visual, audio, source, or generated product asset is needed: this is a\ntooling and directory-boundary contract. Do not create incidental art, icons,\nfonts, textures, sprites, music, sound effects, content, or asset manifests.\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter GATE-0001 approval and implementation, from a clean checkout run the\napproved documented commands for bootstrap, lint, format checking, and the\ndeterministic sample test. Record their command output, the pinned toolchain\nversion, and evidence that the sample test has no clock, RNG, network, browser,\nor player-data dependency. Confirm the directory ownership matches this\nrunbook and that no product or platform lifecycle behaviour was added.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A clean approved-runtime checkout executes the documented bootstrap, lint,\n  format-check, and deterministic sample-test commands successfully.\n- Toolchain and dependency versions are pinned and documented in\n  `docs/development.md`.\n- The shared, web-adapter, PWA-adapter, content, application, and test\n  directories exist with no gameplay or platform lifecycle implementation.\n- The shared core has no browser, timer, network, or renderer dependency.\n- GATE-0001 remains explicit and unresolved; this task does not select its\n  framework, package manager, or CI choices.\n# Factory collection\n\nThis directory contains the runbook-generator's source collection. Read the\nrepository [README](../README.md) for the end-to-end workflow and Supervisor\nconfiguration.\n\n## Source files and roles\n\n| File | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| [F001.md](F001.md)\u2013[F016.md](F016.md) | **F-series factory runbooks.** They turn the brief into a generated project's detailed plan, complete work checklist, and B-series runbook-writing files. |\n| [TEMPLATE.md](TEMPLATE.md) | Required structure for a normal manually written runbook: goal, steps, success checks, and evidence. |\n\nThis directory contains only the reusable factory source. It never contains the\nthousands of runbooks for a particular product.\n\n| Series | Plain-English purpose | Output |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| F-series | The factory: understand the brief, identify the required domains, and design a complete delivery plan. | A workspace in `projects/<slug>/`, including its specification, work checklist, and B-series files. |\n| B-series | The runbook writers: write the next small set of implementation instructions. \u201cBounded\u201d means one B file may write no more than seven R files. | Up to seven R-series files per B task; extra B files are created when more areas need coverage. |\n| R-series | The product-work instructions: describe one small, ordered piece of real work. | Source changes, tests, assets, documentation, or other deliverables required by that R file. |\n\nEvery R-series file must make an explicit asset decision in its front matter:\n`asset_impact: required` with stable comma-separated `asset_ids`, or\n`asset_impact: not_applicable` with a blank `asset_ids`. Asset-required files\nalso describe asset purpose, variants, ownership, original-art constraints, and\nverification in an `## Asset assessment` section. This lets the Supervisor use\nits opt-in asset lane only where the product work genuinely needs it.\n\nEvery R-series file also makes an explicit audio decision: `audio_impact:\nrequired` with stable `audio_ids`, a cue brief, duration, loop policy, and\naudio-style version; or `audio_impact: not_applicable` with the documented\nempty/default values. When required, Supervisor uses the independent local\naudio lane\u2014cue director, ACE-Step 1.5 XL Turbo generator, then technical and\nprovenance QA\u2014before the coding stage. F004 creates an audio-direction and cue\nmap only when the product actually needs music, sound effects, voice, or\naudible feedback.\n\nEvery R-series file must also carry provenance metadata: `source_specifications`\nlinks to the smallest relevant canonical specification sections,\n`source_catalogue_ids` identifies its implementation-catalogue records,\n`authoring_batch` names its B-series writer, and `factory_stages` records the\nF-series stages that established the requirement. This lets an implementation\nagent retrieve focused canonical context without treating procedural F/B files\nas its requirements source.\n\nFor example, a game may eventually have R runbooks for story, combat, content,\nsaves, and platform delivery; an IDE may have R runbooks for language services,\nediting, debugging, and extensions. The F-series derives those areas from the\nbrief rather than assuming either example.\n\n`F001`\u2013`F004` understand the brief and domains, `F005`\u2013`F010` make the delivery\nmap, `F011`\u2013`F013` create and check the specification and work checklist, and\n`F014` creates the first B-series runbook-writing files. When Supervisor runs\nthose B files, each one writes up to seven R-series product-work runbooks. If\nmore R files are required, B dispatcher files create more B files. `F015`\u2013`F016`\nestablish quality and handoff material.\n\n## Run the collection\n\nFrom the repository root, create a named project brief with `supervisor initial`,\nreview `projects/<project-name>/INITIAL.md`, then run:\n\n```zsh\nsupervisor-run --project <project-name>\n```\n\n`--run-initial` runs only `F001`, which is useful for checking that the named\nbrief can be normalized before committing to the full factory. `--project`\nreads that workspace's `INITIAL.md`, runs every F-series factory stage, then\ndynamically follows its registered B-series and R-series collections. It keeps\nstate inside the named workspace and stops only for a real failure or explicit\nreview gate.\n\nGenerated runbook state is kept in the named workspace's `.state/` directory,\nkeeping IDs such as `R0001` isolated between projects. The workspace also owns\nits private `.env`, including its art direction and project-specific Supervisor\nconfiguration. For games, `supervisor initial` also asks for optional music and\nsound direction. Leaving it blank configures Gemma 4 12B to create an original\ndirection and ACE-Step 1.5 XL Turbo as the local audio generator.\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4 | rg '/(B0001|TEMPLATE|F014|implementation-catalogue|03-technical-contract|04-experience-contract)' ; rg -n -C 3 'IMP-000[23]|IMP-0010' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json; sed -n '1,240p' runbooks/TEMPLATE.md; sed -n '1,240p' runbooks/F014.md; sed -n '1,220p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md; sed -n '1,220p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/implementation-catalogue-index.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/B0001.md\n6-  \"first_bounded_domain\": {\"id\": \"SHARD-01\", \"title\": \"Foundation and core contracts\", \"contract_limit\": 250, \"contract_count\": 12, \"state\": \"ready_for_authoring\", \"boundary\": \"Framework-neutral seams, deterministic state/data contracts, local persistence protocol, quality foundations, and original-design controls; excludes campaign rules, UI flows, PWA lifecycle, and release execution.\"},\n7-  \"contracts\": [\n8-    {\"id\":\"IMP-0001\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap reproducible shared project baseline\",\"domain\":\"foundation\",\"targets\":[\"shared\",\"web\",\"pwa\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"NFR-0010\",\"NFR-0012\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"A framework-neutral project skeleton documents one bootstrap, lint, format, and deterministic test command with isolated shared, web-adapter, PWA-adapter, content, and test directories.\",\"prerequisites\":[],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"root build/tooling configuration\",\"src/{core,content,app,platform/web,platform/pwa}/\",\"tests/\",\"docs/development.md\"],\"data\":[\"pinned toolchain declaration\"],\"api\":[\"build/test command interface\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"selecting framework/package manager/CI without DEC-0001\",\"gameplay\",\"service worker\",\"hosting\"],\"verification\":\"Clean approved-runtime checkout runs the documented deterministic sample test, lint, and format checks.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"GATE-0001 / DEC-0001 framework, package-manager, and CI selection before implementation.\",\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"status\":\"gated\"},\n9:    {\"id\":\"IMP-0002\",\"title\":\"Enforce shared-core and adapter dependency seams\",\"domain\":\"foundation\",\"targets\":[\"shared\",\"web\",\"pwa\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"NFR-0010\",\"REQ-0013\",\"NFR-0012\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"A dependency policy and public interfaces prevent core code from importing browser, renderer, clock, network, or platform modules; adapters submit commands and render returned state/events.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0001\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"src/core/public interfaces\",\"src/platform/{web,pwa}/adapter interfaces\",\"architecture dependency rules\",\"docs/architecture.md\"],\"data\":[\"dependency-policy manifest\"],\"api\":[\"Command, state, event, and adapter interfaces\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"concrete combat/exploration/UI\",\"account or network client\",\"target-specific campaign rules\"],\"verification\":\"A dependency-rule test rejects forbidden core imports and a fixture adapter proves command-only interaction.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"None; retain GATE-0001 for tool-specific enforcement.\",\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"status\":\"current\"},\n10:    {\"id\":\"IMP-0003\",\"title\":\"Create deterministic test and fixture harness\",\"domain\":\"tests\",\"targets\":[\"shared\",\"web\",\"pwa\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"NFR-0010\",\"NFR-0012\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"Layered unit, content, integration, and reserved browser-test conventions provide isolated RNG/clock and persistence fixtures with no real service or player data access.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0001\",\"IMP-0002\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"test-runner configuration\",\"tests/{core,content,platform,app}/fixtures\",\"docs/testing.md\"],\"data\":[\"deterministic fixture factories\"],\"api\":[\"test fixture and fake clock/RNG interfaces\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"final feature assertions\",\"real browser/device matrix claims\",\"production telemetry\"],\"verification\":\"Repeated core fixture execution has identical results; a deliberately failing fixture is detected; fixture storage is isolated.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"GATE-0002 before compatibility or performance claims.\",\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"status\":\"current\"},\n11:    {\"id\":\"IMP-0004\",\"title\":\"Define deterministic campaign state and command contract\",\"domain\":\"data\",\"targets\":[\"shared\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0001\",\"REQ-0003\",\"REQ-0004\",\"REQ-0006\",\"NFR-0002\",\"NFR-0010\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"Immutable versioned state, command/result/event envelopes, revision and interaction identifiers, deterministic RNG state, and invariant helpers define legal transition mechanics without feature state machines.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0002\",\"IMP-0003\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"src/core/{state,commands,events,invariants,rng}/\",\"tests/core/contract.*\"],\"data\":[\"campaign snapshot envelope\",\"deterministic RNG state\"],\"api\":[\"Core command/state/event API\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"authored values\",\"combat/inventory/quest algorithms\",\"save driver\",\"UI selection state\",\"account/network state\"],\"verification\":\"Invalid and stale commands do not mutate state; duplicate interaction IDs cause no extra durable transition.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"GATE-0004 before concrete party/content shapes.\",\"complexity\":\"high\",\"status\":\"current\"},\n12:    {\"id\":\"IMP-0005\",\"title\":\"Define versioned content schema and validation boundary\",\"domain\":\"content\",\"targets\":[\"shared\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0002\",\"REQ-0008\",\"NFR-0007\",\"NFR-0008\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"Typed content-package schema and validator cover IDs, references, locale keys, assets, predicates, rewards, and reachability but contain no authored campaign expression.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0001\",\"IMP-0004\",\"IMP-0003\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"src/content/{schema,validator,registry}/\",\"tests/content/validation.*\",\"docs/content-authoring.md\"],\"data\":[\"versioned content package format\",\"asset/provenance manifest schema\"],\"api\":[\"content registry and validator API\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"town/dungeon dialogue/maps/assets\",\"copied reference expression\",\"release packaging\"],\"verification\":\"Valid minimum package passes; each invalid reference class fails; a graph fixture checks opening-to-ending reachability.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"GATE-0004 for quantities, locale, and content detail; human provenance review remains required.\",\"complexity\":\"high\",\"status\":\"current\"},\n13:    {\"id\":\"IMP-0006\",\"title\":\"Specify local save and recovery protocol\",\"domain\":\"persistence\",\"targets\":[\"shared\",\"web\",\"pwa\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0006\",\"REQ-0012\",\"NFR-0002\",\"NFR-0003\",\"NFR-0005\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"A schema protocol specifies immutable revisions, checksums, current-pointer validation, known-good retention, quarantine, separate preferences, deletion confirmation, and safe-boundary persistence rules.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0002\",\"IMP-0004\",\"IMP-0003\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"src/core/save/{schema,compatibility,migrations}/\",\"src/platform/web/persistence/\",\"tests/{core,platform}/save*\",\"docs/save-and-recovery.md\"],\"data\":[\"save snapshot and preference schemas\",\"quarantine record\"],\"api\":[\"snapshot repository and recovery protocol\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"cloud sync\",\"identity\",\"PWA cache records\",\"eviction guarantee\"],\"verification\":\"Fault fixtures cover interrupted write, checksum failure, malformed data, quota/permission failure, quarantine, retry, and selected-slot deletion.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"GATE-0003 / DEC-0003 storage API, quota, support window, and recovery policy.\",\"complexity\":\"high\",\"status\":\"gated\"},\n14-    {\"id\":\"IMP-0007\",\"title\":\"Define pure save migration runner\",\"domain\":\"persistence\",\"targets\":[\"shared\",\"web\",\"pwa\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0006\",\"NFR-0002\",\"NFR-0003\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"Ordered, pure migrations declare supported versions and promote only validated candidates while preserving original invalid bytes for quarantine.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0005\",\"IMP-0006\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"src/core/save/{migrations,compatibility}/\",\"tests/core/save/fixtures\"],\"data\":[\"migration registry and predecessor fixtures\"],\"api\":[\"migration runner API\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"storage driver changes\",\"future data export\",\"automatic destructive repair\"],\"verification\":\"Every declared predecessor migrates once; unsupported or failed migration leaves the original candidate intact and quarantined.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"GATE-0003 / DEC-0003 support-window approval.\",\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"status\":\"gated\"},\n15:    {\"id\":\"IMP-0008\",\"title\":\"Bound P0 identity, authorization, and connected-service exclusions\",\"domain\":\"security\",\"targets\":[\"shared\",\"web\",\"pwa\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0013\",\"REQ-0014\",\"REQ-0015\",\"NFR-0012\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"Architecture and negative contract tests demonstrate that local campaign start/save/resume require no account identifier, token, remote profile, role, entitlement, multiplayer, or safety service.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0002\",\"IMP-0004\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"docs/security-boundary.md\",\"tests/core/connected-scope-exclusion.*\"],\"data\":[\"future connected-service approval checklist\"],\"api\":[\"local-slot identity contract\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"sign-in\",\"OAuth\",\"sync\",\"roles\",\"moderation\",\"purchases\",\"server APIs\"],\"verification\":\"Static scan and negative fixtures find no identity/token/privilege fields or network client in P0 foundation modules.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"GATE-0006 / DEC-0006 before any connected implementation.\",\"complexity\":\"low\",\"status\":\"current\"},\n16:    {\"id\":\"IMP-0009\",\"title\":\"Define privacy-safe local diagnostics boundary\",\"domain\":\"privacy\",\"targets\":[\"web\",\"pwa\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0012\",\"NFR-0006\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"A bounded local diagnostic schema, consent and clear controls, redaction rules, and no-network guardrails support future player recovery without collecting save or dialogue payloads.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0002\",\"IMP-0003\",\"IMP-0006\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"src/platform/web/diagnostics/\",\"src/app/recovery/diagnostic-contract.*\",\"tests/platform/diagnostics.*\",\"docs/privacy-and-recovery.md\"],\"data\":[\"redacted local diagnostic record\",\"consent state\"],\"api\":[\"diagnostic recorder interface\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"diagnostic provider/transport\",\"remote analytics\",\"retention promise\",\"secrets\"],\"verification\":\"Tests prove diagnostics are absent without consent, redact payloads, and can be deleted locally.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"GATE-0005 / DEC-0005 before transport, retention, hosting, or support claims.\",\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"status\":\"gated\"},\n17:    {\"id\":\"IMP-0010\",\"title\":\"Establish semantic accessibility design tokens\",\"domain\":\"accessibility\",\"targets\":[\"shared\",\"web\",\"pwa\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0007\",\"REQ-0010\",\"NFR-0004\",\"NFR-0008\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"Original semantic tokens cover typography, spacing, focus, motion, touch targets, status, contrast, and non-colour cues, enabling later responsive web UI and inherited PWA UI.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0001\",\"IMP-0002\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"src/app/design/tokens/\",\"docs/design-tokens.md\",\"tests/app/design-tokens.*\"],\"data\":[\"semantic token inventory\"],\"api\":[\"token consumption contract\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"screens\",\"maps\",\"final artwork/fonts\",\"final breakpoints\",\"copied reference styling\"],\"verification\":\"Token validation proves semantic labels/non-colour cues and reduced-motion values in representative fixtures.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"GATE-0002 and GATE-0004 before numeric contrast/font/breakpoint claims.\",\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"status\":\"gated\"},\n18-    {\"id\":\"IMP-0011\",\"title\":\"Create original-content provenance control\",\"domain\":\"content\",\"targets\":[\"shared\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0001\",\"REQ-0008\",\"NFR-0008\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"A provenance record format, review checklist, and source-asset manifest make authorship and permitted functional reference scope auditable before authored content enters a build.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0005\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"assets/source-manifest.*\",\"docs/original-content-review.md\",\"tests/content/provenance.*\"],\"data\":[\"provenance manifest and review disposition\"],\"api\":[\"provenance validator input contract\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"creating art/audio/story\",\"licensing approval\",\"automatic legal clearance\"],\"verification\":\"Validator rejects missing provenance fields; human creative review is recorded for each authored package.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"Human creative/provenance review; legal or licensing review if applicable under GATE-0007.\",\"complexity\":\"low\",\"status\":\"current\"},\n19:    {\"id\":\"IMP-0012\",\"title\":\"Document foundation architecture and recovery boundaries\",\"domain\":\"documentation\",\"targets\":[\"shared\",\"web\",\"pwa\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"NFR-0012\",\"NFR-0010\",\"NFR-0002\",\"NFR-0003\"],\"bounded_outcome\":\"Developer documentation records ownership, command flow, save-versus-cache separation, deferred connected scope, and the decisions/gates that later domain authors must preserve.\",\"prerequisites\":[\"IMP-0002\",\"IMP-0004\",\"IMP-0006\",\"IMP-0008\"],\"ownership\":{\"files\":[\"docs/{architecture,development,save-and-recovery,security-boundary}.md\"],\"data\":[\"foundation decision and ownership index\"],\"api\":[\"documented public-boundary inventory\"]},\"forbidden_scope\":[\"player release guide\",\"operational rollout\",\"claiming approved vendor or policy choices\"],\"verification\":\"Documentation review cross-checks every foundation contract's ownership, dependency, and gate against this catalogue.\",\"human_external_gate\":\"None; approval gates are documented, not resolved.\",\"complexity\":\"low\",\"status\":\"current\"}\n20-  ],\n21-  \"expansion_queue\": [\n22-    {\"id\":\"SHARD-02\",\"title\":\"Campaign content and domain logic\",\"max_contracts\":250,\"depends_on\":[\"SHARD-01\"],\"covers\":[\"exploration\",\"dialogue\",\"quests\",\"tutorial\",\"combat\",\"inventory\",\"party progression\",\"town/dungeon/ending content\",\"content validation\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0001\",\"REQ-0002\",\"REQ-0003\",\"REQ-0004\",\"REQ-0005\",\"REQ-0008\",\"NFR-0007\",\"NFR-0008\"],\"gate\":\"GATE-0004\",\"target_allocation\":\"shared core and content only; no UI ownership\",\"state\":\"queued\"},\n--\n25-    {\"id\":\"SHARD-05\",\"title\":\"Connected-scope decision package\",\"max_contracts\":250,\"depends_on\":[\"SHARD-01\"],\"covers\":[\"identity/sync conflict policy\",\"multiplayer/player safety\",\"live operations\",\"commerce/entitlements\"],\"requirement_ids\":[\"REQ-0014\",\"REQ-0015\"],\"gate\":\"GATE-0006\",\"target_allocation\":\"no P0 target implementation; research/decision outputs only if explicitly authorised\",\"state\":\"hold\"}\n26-  ],\n27-  \"requirement_coverage\": {\n28:    \"REQ-0001\":\"queued: SHARD-02; provenance foundation IMP-0011\",\"REQ-0002\":\"queued: SHARD-02\",\"REQ-0003\":\"queued: SHARD-02\",\"REQ-0004\":\"queued: SHARD-02\",\"REQ-0005\":\"queued: SHARD-02 and SHARD-03\",\"REQ-0006\":\"current/gated: IMP-0006, IMP-0007; queued: SHARD-03\",\"REQ-0007\":\"current/gated: IMP-0010; queued: SHARD-03\",\"REQ-0008\":\"current: IMP-0005, IMP-0011; queued: SHARD-02\",\"REQ-0009\":\"queued: SHARD-03\",\"REQ-0010\":\"current/gated: IMP-0010; queued: SHARD-03\",\"REQ-0011\":\"queued: SHARD-04\",\"REQ-0012\":\"current/gated: IMP-0006, IMP-0009; queued: SHARD-03 and SHARD-04\",\"REQ-0013\":\"current: IMP-0002, IMP-0008\",\"REQ-0014\":\"gated: SHARD-05 / GATE-0006; no P0 implementation\",\"REQ-0015\":\"hold: SHARD-05 / GATE-0006; no P0 implementation\",\"NFR-0001\":\"queued: SHARD-04\",\"NFR-0002\":\"current/gated: IMP-0004, IMP-0006, IMP-0007\",\"NFR-0003\":\"current/gated: IMP-0006, IMP-0007; queued: SHARD-04\",\"NFR-0004\":\"current/gated: IMP-0010; queued: SHARD-03\",\"NFR-0005\":\"current/gated: IMP-0006; queued: SHARD-03\",\"NFR-0006\":\"current/gated: IMP-0009; queued: SHARD-04\",\"NFR-0007\":\"current: IMP-0005; queued: SHARD-02\",\"NFR-0008\":\"current: IMP-0005, IMP-0010, IMP-0011; queued: SHARD-02\",\"NFR-0009\":\"gated: SHARD-04 / GATE-0002\",\"NFR-0010\":\"current: IMP-0001, IMP-0002, IMP-0003, IMP-0004; queued target adapters in SHARD-03 and SHARD-04\",\"NFR-0011\":\"gated: SHARD-04 / GATE-0005\",\"NFR-0012\":\"current/gated: IMP-0001, IMP-0002, IMP-0003, IMP-0008, IMP-0012; queued: SHARD-04\"\n29-  },\n30:  \"dependency_graph\": {\"nodes\":[\"IMP-0001\",\"IMP-0002\",\"IMP-0003\",\"IMP-0004\",\"IMP-0005\",\"IMP-0006\",\"IMP-0007\",\"IMP-0008\",\"IMP-0009\",\"IMP-0010\",\"IMP-0011\",\"IMP-0012\"],\"acyclic\":true,\"validation\":\"Each prerequisite points only to an earlier numbered contract; SHARD dependencies only point from later shards to earlier shards.\"}\n31-}\n---\ntask_id: T001\nsequence: 1\ntitle: Describe one small, reviewable change\nbrowser_impact: none\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nDescribe the smallest complete outcome. State what is in scope and, equally\nimportant, what must not be changed.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- The intended behaviour is implemented and documented where it affects users or developers.\n- Focused automated tests cover the change and pass.\n- No unrelated refactors, network calls, or credentials are introduced.\n---\ntask_id: F014\nsequence: 14\ntitle: Generate the bounded implementation-runbook authoring collection\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nGenerate only the bootstrap wave of ordinary Supervisor runbooks in\n`projects/<project-slug>/authoring-runbooks/`: a bounded catalogue-expansion\ntask followed by a dispatcher task. The dispatcher allocates at most ten\nauthoring B-series tasks per wave, creates one successor dispatcher, and stops.\nEach authoring task instructs Codex to create only its assigned R-series\nimplementation runbooks under\n`projects/<project-slug>/runbooks/`, then validate their metadata and update the\nmanifest. The successor dispatcher repeats this process until no queued\ncatalogue chapter or unassigned implementation contract remains. Generate an\n`INITIAL.md` context document in `authoring-runbooks/`\nthat points to the canonical `PROJECT_BRIEF.md` and specification.\n\nRegister the generated authoring collection with the source collection by\nwriting `runbooks/.supervisor-children/<project-slug>.json` containing\n`{\"runbooks_dir\": \"../projects/<project-slug>/authoring-runbooks\"}`. Also\nwrite `authoring-runbooks/.supervisor-children/implementation.json` containing\n`{\"runbooks_dir\": \"../runbooks\"}`. These are explicit child-collection\nregistrations; `supervisor-run --run-all` follows them automatically after the\nparent collection is accepted.\n\nEvery dispatcher first expands at most one queued specification/catalogue domain\nchapter (maximum 250 `IMP-*` contracts), validates its coverage, then allocates\nready contracts. If any queued chapter or ready contract remains, it creates no\nmore than ten authoring tasks and exactly one successor dispatcher. If none\nremains, it creates the final audit task instead. This is a durable loop, not a\nrecursive prompt or an unbounded single run.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must use the standard Supervisor front matter\nand contain: precise objective; dependencies; relevant requirement IDs; owned\nfiles/data/interfaces; required behaviour; non-goals; platform differences;\nverification commands/evidence; and observable acceptance criteria. It must be\nsmall enough for one implementation task, not a vague epic.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must retain machine-readable provenance to the\ncanonical decisions that justify it. Its front matter must declare all of these\nnon-empty fields, using comma-separated values where more than one applies:\n\n```yaml\nsource_specifications: specification/03-technical-contract.md#save-state\nsource_catalogue_ids: IMP-SAVE-001\nauthoring_batch: B0007\nfactory_stages: F003,F005,F012,F013\n```\n\n`source_specifications` points to the smallest relevant canonical\nspecification section; `source_catalogue_ids` identifies the allocated\nimplementation-catalogue records; `authoring_batch` identifies the B-series\nwriter that created the R file; and `factory_stages` identifies the F-series\nstages that established the requirement. This is a traceability trail, not an\ninstruction to inject every F or B runbook into the implementation agent's\nprompt. The R task uses these links to read only the canonical context it needs.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must also include a detailed asset assessment.\nIts front matter must always declare both fields below, even when no assets are\nneeded:\n\n```yaml\nasset_impact: required # or not_applicable\nasset_ids: village_gate_main,village_gate_construction # blank only when not_applicable\n```\n\nFor `asset_impact: required`, the B-series writer must reserve stable,\nproduct-specific asset IDs (never generic names such as `image1`), provide an\n`asset_brief` and `visual_style_version`, and include an `## Asset assessment`\nsection explaining the assets' purpose, where they appear, the required visual\nstates/variants, source-of-truth ownership, and original-art acceptance checks.\nFor `not_applicable`, that section must explain why the scope needs no generated\nor source visual asset and explicitly prohibit incidental asset creation. This\nkeeps UI, illustration, icon, texture, sprite, audio-visual, and other product\nasset work visible in the implementation plan instead of discovering it midway\nthrough coding.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must also declare an audio assessment, even\nwhen audio is not needed:\n\n```yaml\naudio_impact: required # or not_applicable\naudio_ids: ember_menu_loop # blank only when not_applicable\naudio_brief: 30-second seamless instrumental menu cue # blank only when not_applicable\naudio_duration_seconds: 30 # 0 only when not_applicable\naudio_loop: required # required, not_required, or not_applicable\naudio_style_version: project-audio-v1\n```\n\nFor `audio_impact: required`, the writer must reserve stable cue IDs, cite the\nrelevant `04-audio-direction.md` section, describe the in-product trigger and\nmix/accessibility expectations, and assign a sensible duration and loop policy.\nThe Supervisor's separate local audio lane then creates a manifest, invokes\nACE-Step, and performs technical/provenance QA before coding begins. For\n`not_applicable`, every audio field has the stated empty/default form so an\notherwise unrelated task cannot silently generate music or sound effects.\n\nEach B-series authoring task may create at most seven R-series implementation\nrunbooks. It must stop after its assigned seven (or fewer), update the manifest,\nand leave remaining work for the next bounded batch.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- The initial collection contains only a bounded bootstrap wave; each\n  dispatcher expands at most one 250-contract domain shard, creates at most ten\n  new B-series authoring tasks plus one successor, then exits. It never attempts\n  to materialise the whole game plan.\n- There is exactly one valid B-series authoring runbook per allocated manifest\n  batch, and it creates no more than seven assigned R-series runbooks.\n- B-series runbooks never overwrite an existing R-series contract with a\n  conflicting ID; they stop and report mismatches.\n- Every generated B-series runbook includes its context packet, output list,\n  validation procedure, checkpoint/update procedure, and a clear stop condition.\n- Both child-collection registration files exist, contain valid JSON with a\n  relative `runbooks_dir`, and point to the generated authoring and\n  implementation collections respectively.\n- R-series contracts use stable IDs and parse with the Supervisor runbook\n  loader; their declared dependencies match the manifest, every contract has\n  explicit `asset_impact` and `asset_ids` metadata, and asset-required\n  contracts have non-empty stable IDs. Every R contract also has non-empty,\n  valid `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, `authoring_batch`,\n  and `factory_stages` provenance metadata.\n- Every R-series contract has explicit valid audio metadata. Audio-required\n  contracts have stable IDs, a non-empty cue brief, duration, loop policy,\n  audio-style version, and a source link to the canonical audio direction.\n- The collection contains a final audit B-series task after all authoring\n  batches and catalogue expansions are complete, rather than pretending that\n  thousands of contracts can be reviewed in one generation step.\n# 03 \u2014 Technical, data, trust, and quality contract\n\n**Inputs:** [`INITIAL.md`](../INITIAL.md), [product brief](01-product-brief.md),\nand [feature model](02-feature-model.md). This contract makes the P0 offline\nsingle-player release implementable without selecting a vendor or creating a\ndependency on the contradictory connected-service requests. It is a design\ncontract, not permission to collect data or operate services.\n\n## Architecture and requirement traceability\n\nThe production architecture is a static, client-side 2D web game: a shared\ndeterministic domain core, browser presentation/input adapters, versioned\nauthored content, and local durable storage. The web host may deliver a signed\nrelease bundle and service worker, but it is not a campaign authority. The\ncore must have no browser APIs, timers, network calls, or renderer dependencies.\n\n| Component / boundary | Requirement served | Contract |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Game shell and responsive renderer | J1, J3, J6; readable mobile-to-desktop play | Owns navigation, focus/visibility pause, semantic UI, visual settings, and rendering only. It requests domain commands and renders returned state/events. |\n| Input adapters | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad accessibility | Convert platform events into a shared, remappable `InputAction` vocabulary; unavailable hardware is non-fatal. No adapter may mutate campaign state directly. |\n| Deterministic rules core | Tutorial combat, quests, inventory, progression | Owns legal state transitions, seeded/randomness policy, combat resolution, reward ledger, and invariants. Command validation precedes every mutation. |\n| Content registry and validator | One town/dungeon, original content, localisation | Loads the active versioned content package; resolves IDs/keys and rejects invalid packages before play. It is read-only at runtime. |\n| Save/recovery repository | J5, long-session recovery | Owns atomic local snapshot writes, validation, quarantine, migration, retention, export/import when approved, and explicit deletion. |\n| PWA release manager | J7, install/offline/update safety | Owns precache manifest, cache integrity, install affordance, and safe-boundary activation. It never replaces a running release or a pending save write. |\n| Error boundary and consent-gated diagnostics | Crash/error recovery and privacy | Shows retry/title/recovery actions. Local diagnostic records are minimal and removable; no telemetry transport exists in P0. |\n\n### Dependency direction and state rules\n\n```text\nauthored content \u2500\u2500> content validator \u2500\u2500> deterministic rules core <\u2500\u2500 input actions\n                                            \u2502\nweb/PWA shell + renderer <\u2500\u2500 state/events \u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500> save/recovery repository\n        \u2502                                   \u2502\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500> PWA release manager \u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500> error boundary / local diagnostics\n```\n\nOnly the rules core can produce durable campaign state. The shell owns\nephemeral UI state (open panels, selection, animation), while the repository\nowns only serialized snapshots and recovery metadata. Rendering, input,\ncaching, and diagnostics must not be required to make a legal game decision.\n\n## Data contract\n\n### Ownership, lifecycle, and retention\n\n| Data class | Owner and lifecycle | Persistence / retention | Access and recovery |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Content package | Build/content owner: authored \u2192 schema-validated \u2192 bundled \u2192 immutable release artifact \u2192 superseded | Public static assets; current and previous known-good cache retained per cache policy | Read by client only; invalid references reject package before gameplay. |\n| Campaign snapshot | Player; new game \u2192 checkpoint/manual save \u2192 valid, migrated, quarantined, or explicitly deleted | Browser-origin local storage appropriate for structured durable data (implementation selects IndexedDB or equivalent); retain current valid slot plus last-known-good checkpoint | Same browser origin only in P0; player may load, delete after confirmation, and receive export/support guidance where feasible. |\n| Settings | Player; default \u2192 previewed \u2192 applied \u2192 persisted/reset | Separate local preference record; current session remains active if persistence fails | Read/write only by local game; reset requires confirmation. |\n| Recovery metadata | Client; write attempt/failure \u2192 recovery offer \u2192 resolved | Local, bounded and tied to save slot; remove with slot/delete-local-data action | Never overwrites valid snapshot; aids quarantine and fallback. |\n| Diagnostic record | Player-controlled, consent-gated; fault \u2192 local record \u2192 optional future export/delete | Bounded local record, no identifiers, no campaign dialogue, and no network sending in P0 | Player can decline, clear, or export only after a future approved workflow. |\n\nThe local campaign is the P0 source of truth. There is no account, remote\nprofile, analytics stream, purchase ledger, match state, or cloud backup.\nRemote account-linked replication is a later optional mirror\u2014not campaign truth\nuntil approval defines identity, vendor, consent, cost, retention, deletion,\nsupport, conflict UX, and outage behaviour.\n\n### Versioned content and save schemas\n\nAll high-volume authored data uses a versioned, machine-validated package:\n\n```text\nContentPackage { packageId, semanticVersion, schemaVersion, localeDefault,\n  scenes[], exits[], dialogues[], quests[], encounters[], enemyDefinitions[],\n  itemDefinitions[], rewards[], localisation[], assetManifest[] }\nSaveSnapshot { schemaVersion, contentPackageId, contentVersion, slotId,\n  revision, checkpointId, campaign, quests, party, inventory, rewardLedger,\n  settingsReference?, checksum, committedAt }\n```\n\n- **Ownership and authoring:** designated game/content owners maintain source\n  data in repository-controlled files and original asset references. The exact\n  editor/format is an implementation choice; generated bundles are derived,\n  never hand-edited.\n- **Validation:** CI validates JSON/schema shape, unique stable IDs, required\n  fields, asset and localisation-key existence, valid quest predicates,\n  scene/spawn/exit targets, legal reward/item/enemy references, no duplicate\n  reward IDs, and reachability from opening scene to ending. It also rejects\n  missing required translations in any release locale and unsafe quest-item\n  removal paths.\n- **Relationship rules:** every required quest path has a reachable transition;\n  each locked exit predicate refers to an existing quest state; each encounter\n  has legal targets/actions and a completion path; all rewards resolve to\n  extant definitions. Tests include valid minimal package, each broken\n  reference class, and a graph traversal proving the authored campaign is\n  completable.\n- **Import/export:** P0 imports content only at build time through the\n  validator and packages it into the release. Player save export/import is not\n  promised; if added, it must use a versioned envelope, parse/validate before\n  write, never replace a valid save until confirmation, and reject untrusted\n  payloads safely.\n- **Compatibility:** each release declares supported content and save schema\n  ranges. Incompatible saves are quarantined with their original bytes and a\n  clear fallback/new-game action; a migration must create a new candidate and\n  validate it before promotion.\n\n## Commands, validation, idempotency, and concurrency\n\nThe core exposes typed intent commands such as `StartCampaign`,\n`ChooseAction`, `UseItem`, `Talk`, `AcceptQuest`, `ClaimReward`,\n`EnterExit`, `CommitCheckpoint`, `LoadSlot`, and `ApplySettings`. A command\ncontains an expected campaign revision and an interaction/action ID. The core\nreturns either a typed rejection without mutation or an ordered state/event\nresult with a new revision.\n\n- Validate session state, command shape, actor/target existence, availability,\n  eligibility, quest predicate, and expected revision before mutation. UI\n  selections are untrusted/stale inputs even though all execution is local.\n- Use stable `rewardId`/`grantId` ledger entries and interaction IDs. Retrying\n  an acknowledged reward, quest completion, or checkpoint returns the prior\n  result rather than granting twice.\n- One command is processed at a time per active campaign. Disable/debounce\n  duplicate controls while pending; compare expected revision on completion.\n  Visibility changes, gamepad repeats, and multiple tabs cannot interleave\n  transitions. A second tab must be warned/read-only or acquire an explicit\n  local slot lease; stale writers fail without replacing the latest revision.\n- Combat resolution uses an explicit deterministic seed/state recorded at\n  safe boundaries. It never persists an unresolved turn; a crash restores the\n  prior checkpoint, avoiding half-consumed items, half-applied damage, or\n  duplicate rewards.\n\n## Persistence, migration, and failure semantics\n\nSave commit is a write-ahead replacement protocol: serialize a complete\nsnapshot \u2192 validate checksum/schema/invariants \u2192 write candidate under a new\nrevision \u2192 verify read-back \u2192 mark current \u2192 retain prior known-good snapshot.\nImplementations must use browser storage semantics that make the final pointer\nswitch atomic, or emulate it with immutable revision records and a validated\ncurrent pointer. Never clear the prior save before the new pointer is valid.\n\n| Condition | Required behaviour |\n| --- | --- |\n| Storage quota, permission, or unavailable local storage | Keep session playable; show non-blocking save failure and retry guidance; do not claim persistence succeeded. |\n| Interrupted/crashed write | Ignore incomplete candidate, retain/reload prior confirmed snapshot, and show recovery state at next launch. |\n| Malformed/checksum-invalid save | Quarantine bytes and metadata; do not overwrite; offer last valid checkpoint/new game and support/export guidance where feasible. |\n| Migration failure or unsupported version | Preserve original, fail closed into quarantine, and offer clear version/fallback choices. |\n| Bad content package/reference | Fail build/CI; if discovered at runtime, stop the affected route, retain save, and offer title/retry rather than corrupting campaign state. |\n| Cache/network refresh failure | Continue with known-good cached release; explain first-install offline limitation without pretending an uncached release is playable. |\n| Application fault/crash loop | Error boundary offers retry, title, and safe recovery; diagnostics remain local unless explicitly consented and a future transport is approved. |\n\nMigration functions are ordered, pure transformations from one explicit schema\nversion to the next, with fixtures for every supported predecessor. They must\nbe idempotent or record the source version so a retry cannot apply a migration\ntwice. A release may remove old migrations only after a separately approved\nsupport/retention decision.\n\n## Trust, privacy, and security\n\n- Treat content, imported saves, URL parameters, browser events, service-worker\n  messages, and persisted bytes as untrusted. Validate and bound sizes before\n  parsing; never execute content as code or render dialogue via unsafe HTML.\n- Serve production assets over HTTPS with a restrictive content security policy,\n  no third-party scripts by default, dependency lock/review, and build-time\n  asset integrity/hash manifest. Do not embed credentials in the client.\n- Use same-origin storage isolation; minimise persisted data to game state and\n  chosen preferences. Do not collect names, contact data, precise location,\n  advertising IDs, account identifiers, payment data, or gameplay analytics in\n  P0.\n- Diagnostics default off where consent is required and must state fields,\n  purpose, retention, deletion, and transport before collection. A local\n  diagnostic record excludes free-text dialogue and complete save contents.\n- There is no authorization role in P0 because there is no server resource or\n  identity boundary. Locally, a player controls their browser profile; this is\n  not a security guarantee against a device owner. Any server feature requires\n  explicit least-privilege roles, authenticated authorization on every request,\n  rate limits, audit events, deletion/retention policy, incident ownership, and\n  documented offline/outage behaviour before implementation.\n- No copied branding, assets, text, layouts, or distinctive interaction\n  sequences enter the content pipeline. Provenance/review is required for\n  shipped art, audio, writing, and dependencies.\n\n## Target-platform matrix\n\n| Contract area | Responsive public web application | Installable PWA |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Shared code/data boundary | Same rules core, content package, save schema, semantic actions, accessibility settings, and release compatibility rules. Browser adapter owns viewport/input differences. | Same shared core/data. Service worker, installation, cache lifecycle, and platform install affordance are PWA adapters. |\n| Input and device capability | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where exposed; feature-detect and show available mappings. Handle resize, rotation, focus/visibility, reduced-motion/contrast settings where available. | Same inputs and feature detection; no assumed hardware merely because installed. Installation, notifications, or storage-persistence prompts are optional and must not block play. |\n| Offline and recovery | May play offline only after relevant assets are cached. First uncached visit tells player an initial download/network is required. Local save protocol applies. | Primary offline route after successful install/cache. Precaches approved shell/content, retains known-good cache on refresh failure, and keeps saves independent of cache eviction. |\n| Build and test | Produce hashed static assets, manifest, and browser-compatible bundle. Test core/unit/content validation, responsive layouts, input adaptations, save corruption/migration, error states, and supported browser/device/network matrix. | Build includes service worker and install manifest. Test installability, offline reload, cache rollback/update-at-safe-boundary, storage pressure, and all shared checks. |\n| Accessibility and performance | Keyboard-operable menus, semantic labels, visible focus, readable scalable text, captions, non-colour cues, settings persistence, responsive phone/tablet/desktop checks. Establish approval-gated performance budgets for initial load, interaction frame time, memory, bundle/cache size, and slow-network behaviour before release. | Same accessibility/performance requirements, plus offline messaging, update state, install prompt alternatives, and no inaccessible full-screen/install-only path. Measure cold/warm/offline startup and cache size. |\n| Release channel and policy | Immutable versioned static release to approved web hosting; staged/canary and rollback process must be selected before launch. Publish supported-browser matrix, privacy notice if diagnostics changes, and recovery notes. | Browser-mediated install/update channel; manifest/icon/display policy and service-worker behaviour must meet supported browser/platform rules. Prompt only at safe boundaries; release notes and rollback retain prior runnable cache. |\n\nSupport targets are expressed as capability tests rather than invented minimum\nOS versions: current and previous major releases of agreed evergreen browsers\non representative phone, tablet, and desktop classes; exact versions/devices,\nbrowser engine exclusions, storage quota, and slow-network thresholds remain a\nrelease approval gate.\n\n## Observability, quality evidence, and release verification\n\nP0 observability is local and player-respecting: structured console/build logs\nin development, release/version/cache status visible in support UI, and an\noptional bounded local fault record. It has no remote monitoring dependency.\nEvery fault has a player-facing recovery path and a test that asserts retained\nsave/cache behaviour as applicable.\n\n| Evidence layer | Required checks |\n| --- | --- |\n| Rules and data | Unit/property tests for legal/illegal commands, revision rejection, idempotent grants, deterministic combat fixtures, save encode/decode/checksum, migration fixtures, recovery/quarantine, and content schema/reachability/reference validation. |\n| Integration | Test shell-to-core action mapping, checkpoint ordering, focus/pause, unavailable input fallback, settings persistence failure, cache/save independence, and PWA safe update behaviour. |\n| Target/browser | Run agreed browser/device matrix for keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad-adapted paths, responsive viewport checks, offline first/repeat visit, install flow where supported, storage failure, and browser accessibility audit/manual assistive-technology checks. |\n| Performance/security | Capture approved load/frame/memory/cache budgets on representative low-capability and slow-network profiles; dependency/license/provenance review, CSP/header check, no-secret scan, and import/parser fuzz/bounds tests. |\n| Release gate | Versioned content/save compatibility declaration, clean content validation, migration/recovery suite, PWA offline/update/rollback check, accessibility and performance results, privacy/diagnostic confirmation, and tested rollback to a known-good release. |\n\nRelease is blocked when validation cannot prove campaign reachability, a save\ncould overwrite the last known-good state, an update can activate mid-combat or\nmid-write, an unsupported capability blocks the core path without explanation,\nor any mandatory quality budget/matrix remains unapproved or unmeasured.\n\n## Decisions deferred for approval\n\nThese are intentionally not selected by this contract: game engine/framework,\nrendering library, static host/CDN, CI provider, diagnostics vendor/transport,\nexact storage API/quota, browser/OS/device minimums, performance thresholds,\nlanguages, age/parental/regional obligations, service-worker rollout policy,\nand content editor format. Account identity, remote save replication,\nmultiplayer/player safety, live operations, analytics, purchases, and\nentitlements require separate product, security, privacy, cost, failure-mode,\n# 04 \u2014 Experience and accessibility contract\n\n**Inputs:** [product brief](01-product-brief.md), [feature model](02-feature-model.md), and [technical, data, trust, and quality contract](03-technical-contract.md). This is the experience contract for the P0 offline campaign: one town, one dungeon, turn-based combat, inventory, quests, saves, settings, and a short ending. It specifies behaviour and presentation without choosing an engine, a library, exact art assets, or an unapproved connected service.\n\n## Experience premise and originality boundary\n\nThe player arrives as a wayfinder in **Larkspur Reach**, a small hillside town whose old beacon has gone dark. A local request leads through the nearby **Glassroot Hollow** to relight it. This is a compact, hopeful mystery about attention, repair, and returning safely\u2014not a borrowed world, cast, plot, place, or visual identity. The ending is a quiet return to the town with the beacon restored and the campaign marked complete.\n\nThe visual language is original: warm hand-painted fantasy with broad, readable silhouettes; worn clay, moss, lantern-gold, plum shadow, and rain-blue accents; gently textured scenery; and simple, high-contrast interface planes. Characters, creature designs, maps, names, dialogue, quest structure, UI composition, animation timing, sound, and gameplay choreography must be independently authored. The functional reference informs only the broad capability of a party-based, turn-based adventure. It must not inform trade dress, screen layouts, menus, copy, world-building, assets, or distinctive sequences.\n\n## Information architecture and navigation\n\nThe shell has a persistent, semantic navigation model. The game view is the primary workspace; panels are temporary layers rather than separate unexplained destinations.\n\n```text\nLaunch / title\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 New game \u2192 accessibility quick setup (skippable) \u2192 tutorial \u2192 town \u2192 dungeon \u2192 ending\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Continue / Load \u2192 slot picker \u2192 restored safe boundary \u2192 game view\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Settings \u2192 accessibility, controls, audio, display, language (when supplied)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Help & controls \u2192 input-specific action reference\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Save data \u2192 load, delete-confirmation, recovery status\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Privacy & diagnostics \u2192 local-data explanation, consent / clear actions\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 PWA status \u2192 install guidance, offline readiness, safe update state\n\nGame view\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 objective / quest panel\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 inventory and party panels\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 pause menu \u2192 save, settings, help, return to title\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 contextual interaction / dialogue / combat layers\n```\n\n- The title exposes only available actions. With no valid save, `Continue` and `Load` are absent or disabled with the accompanying explanation; `New game` remains the primary action.\n- `Escape`, a visible pause affordance, and the mapped gamepad pause action open pause at any safe playable state. Focus loss or tab hiding pauses the game and explains this only once per session.\n- Every panel has a visible title, close control, and a known return target. Closing restores the prior selection and scroll position where feasible. Browser Back never silently discards progress: it closes a transient panel first; leaving the game prompts only when an unsaved, recoverable change would be lost.\n- Modal layers are reserved for blocking choices: save deletion, settings reset, a new-game replacement of an existing active slot, and a recovery choice. They state consequence, affected slot/data, irreversible status, and a safe default (`Cancel`). No timed dialog expires.\n- Navigation labels use player language: `Quest`, `Bag`, `Party`, `Save`, `Settings`, and `Help`. Icons always have adjacent text or an accessible name; an icon alone is never the only cue.\n\n## Story, content, and feedback principles\n\nWriting is concise, warm, and grounded. Dialogue advances in short, skimmable turns: speaker name, one thought or action, then a clear response/continue choice. Quest copy answers **what happened**, **what to do next**, and **where to go** without hiding required direction in decorative prose. The tutorial teaches one action at a time: move/interact, select a combat action and target, observe the result, then save. It never assumes prior genre knowledge.\n\nNarrative presentation gives players control: advance, review the current exchange, and enable captions; it does not auto-advance essential text. A quest update is both spoken/written in the panel and visibly confirmed near the objective. Victory, gained item, blocked route, invalid action, save completion, update-ready, and recovery messages use a combination of short text, an icon or shape, optional audio/haptic feedback where the platform supports it, and a non-blocking visual change. Colour and sound are supplementary, never the sole signal.\n\nCombat is an ordered decision loop: announce whose turn it is, offer legal actions, show required target selection, then resolve with an interruptible/reduced-motion-friendly result and return to the next decision. Illegal or unavailable choices explain why and preserve the current turn. Exploration interactions use an explicit prompt with the action name; locked routes name the missing condition rather than merely refusing movement.\n\n## Layout, visual tokens, and responsive behaviour\n\nThe game scene retains its 2D world view while the interface reflows around it. Essential information is never permanently covered by a decorative frame, cut off by device notches, or dependent on hover.\n\n| Token / rule | Contract |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--surface-base`, `--surface-raised` | Warm parchment/dark-plum interface planes. Body text and essential icons meet at least **4.5:1** contrast; large text (at least 24 CSS px regular or 18.66 CSS px bold) meets **3:1**. |\n| `--text-primary`, `--text-muted` | Primary is used for all essential content. Muted text is never the only carrier of an instruction, state, or warning and meets 4.5:1 when conveying necessary text. |\n| `--accent-action`, `--state-success/warning/danger` | Accent is paired with text and a distinct icon/shape. Focus does not rely on the action colour. Error and destructive states use wording plus iconography. |\n| `--focus-ring` | A high-visibility 3 CSS-px minimum outline with at least 3:1 contrast against adjacent colours; never removed. It has clear offset from the focused control. |\n| Typography | Sans-serif UI type with clear letterforms; text scaling is supported to 200% without clipping, overlap, horizontal page scrolling, or lost actions. Body/default dialogue begins at a readable base size; users may choose larger text and line spacing. |\n| Spacing and targets | Minimum 44 by 44 CSS px target for touch-operable controls, with 8 CSS px separation where adjacent targets could be confused. Targets are not made smaller on compact screens. |\n| Motion | Motion is brief, non-essential, and can be reduced. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; provide an in-game reduction setting that removes camera shake, parallax, flashes, auto-panning, and nonessential transitions. No essential timing depends on animation. |\n\n| Viewport | Layout behaviour |\n| --- | --- |\n| Phone / narrow portrait | Full-width world view with safe-area insets. Objective is a compact expandable summary; action controls form a bottom reachable sheet. Inventory, quest, settings, and dialogue become full-screen panels with a sticky close/back control. Combat actions stack or use a single-column selection list; no critical control is below the browser chrome. |\n| Tablet / medium | World stays primary with a side or bottom contextual panel. Combat has a clear action area and target area side-by-side when space allows; otherwise it uses the phone sequence. Panels never overlap the action prompt. |\n| Desktop / wide | Centred game stage with persistent but collapsible objective/status rail. Inventory and quest can use a two-column inspection layout; combat keeps actions and party/enemy status simultaneously visible. Mouse hover may preview a definition but never supplies essential information. |\n\nRotation, resize, zoom, virtual keyboards, and PWA display-mode changes preserve the current game state and selection where practical. If reflow temporarily obscures the selected control, focus moves to the panel heading and a short status message announces the layout change. Full screen is optional and provides an obvious exit path.\n\n## Input and interaction adaptation\n\nAll supported physical inputs invoke one semantic action vocabulary: move, inspect/interact, confirm, cancel/pause, open quest, open bag, open settings, navigate previous/next, and choose a legal combat action/target. A player can complete every core journey without pointer precision, dragging, multi-touch, rapid repetition, or a time limit.\n\n| Surface / input | Required adaptation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Keyboard | Arrow keys and WASD move/navigate where appropriate; Tab and Shift+Tab move through UI controls in visual reading order; Enter/Space confirm; Escape cancels/closes/pause. Avoid keyboard traps; provide an early `Skip to game controls` link from the shell. Key repeat must not confirm a destructive choice or consume multiple turn actions. Control mappings are visible and remappable subject to the later-approved configuration depth; conflicts are explained before saving. |\n| Mouse / trackpad | Click targets match the semantic controls; hover is optional enrichment only. Right-click is not required. The pointer never needs to trace tiny map details to perform a required interaction. |\n| Touch | On-screen controls are reachable, labelled, and may be toggled in settings. Use tap-to-confirm and explicit target selection; do not require pinch, swipe-only navigation, double tap, drag, or long press. A touch action has the same feedback as its keyboard equivalent. |\n| Gamepad | Feature-detect rather than promise a model. Show button glyphs only after detection and accompany them with action text. D-pad/left stick navigates; primary face button confirms; secondary face button cancels; menu button pauses; shoulder buttons may switch top-level panels when discoverable. A disconnected controller preserves state and presents keyboard/mouse/touch alternatives without blocking. |\n| Remote / focus-only devices | These are not a selected delivery target. If a browser presents only directional/confirm/back focus input, the menus use the gamepad-style focus order and all core controls remain reachable; continuous exploration is not promised until a target/device decision is approved. |\n| Voice and other assistive input | Voice command recognition is not collected, enabled, or required in P0. Controls expose standard semantic HTML roles, names, states, and operable buttons so browser voice control, switch control, eye tracking, and other assistive input can target visible actions by label. Canvas/world interactions have equivalent labelled UI prompts and action-list routes. |\n\n## Accessibility policy\n\nThe release target is WCAG 2.2 AA for the public UI and the operable game controls, evaluated with automated checks and manual keyboard, screen-reader, zoom/reflow, touch, and reduced-motion review on the approved browser/device matrix. This is a product quality target; exact assistive-technology combinations remain a release approval gate rather than an invented compatibility claim.\n\n- Use semantic landmarks for banner/title navigation, main game controls, complementary objective/status, and dialogs. Mark decorative art as hidden from assistive technology. Give the world canvas a concise labelled description and provide its essential current state through adjacent semantic status and action elements.\n- Every interactive element has a unique accessible name that matches its visible label where one exists. Examples: `Open quest: Relight the Beacon`, `Use Moss Tonic on Rowan`, `Close inventory`, `Save slot 1`, and `Delete slot 1`.\n- Dialogues announce speaker and text through a controlled, non-interrupting status mechanism; combat announces turn start, selected action/target, damage/healing, and victory/defeat without flooding announcements for decorative animation frames. Notifications are concise, deduplicated, and reviewable in the status/quest panel.\n- Focus is visible at all times, enters a dialog at its heading or first safe action, remains contained only while that blocking dialog is open, and returns to its invoking control when closed. A newly opened non-modal panel receives focus on its heading. Error focus moves to an actionable summary, not an arbitrary visual element.\n- Do not use flicker above three flashes per second. Provide captions/subtitles for all non-text audio that conveys story, instruction, combat state, or warning; identify speaker and relevant sound in concise text. Master audio, music, effects, caption visibility, text size, contrast, and motion settings are reachable before starting a new game and while paused.\n- Required state has a text equivalent: party health uses values and state wording as well as bars; enemy intent uses a label/icon; map interaction, quest progression, and save status use text. Visual settings include a higher-contrast mode and non-colour state markers.\n- Users can pause, slow, or disable nonessential movement and screen effects. No essential dialogue, battle selection, confirmation, error message, or recovery choice disappears by timeout.\n- Settings save independently of campaign saves where storage permits. If persistence fails, keep the in-session choice, state that it will not persist, and preserve a usable default. Reset settings is confirmed and describes the affected preference categories.\n\n## Empty, error, recovery, destructive, and privacy-sensitive states\n\n| Situation | Experience requirement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Empty inventory / no quest / no save | State what is empty, why it may be empty, and the next useful action: e.g., `No items yet\u2014explore the town and finish the first encounter.` No decorative blank panels. |\n| Invalid action, locked exit, unavailable target | Keep the player\u2019s state and turn intact; name the unmet requirement and return focus to the legal choices. Do not use a generic `Error` as the only explanation. |\n| Storage failure, corrupt save, or interrupted save | Never claim a save completed until confirmed. Retain the last valid checkpoint; show retry, return-to-title, and fallback/new-game paths. Quarantined saves are explained as preserved but unusable; recovery text avoids blame. |\n| Offline, install, cache, or update state | Explain whether the current cached version can play, whether an initial download is required, and that an update will wait for a save-safe title/menu boundary. Declining install or update does not block play on a valid cached/current release. |\n| Fault / crash recovery | Present plain-language retry, return to title, and safe-recovery actions. Preserve confirmed progress. A diagnostic option is optional and clearly separate from recovery. |\n| Delete a save | Require a confirmation dialog that names the slot, its progress/time where available, and that deletion is irreversible. Initial focus is `Cancel`; `Delete` is visually and semantically distinct and cannot be activated through a stale/repeated input. Only the selected local slot and associated recovery metadata are removed. |\n| New game over an active slot / reset settings | Explain precisely what will change. New game must use a separate slot or obtain explicit confirmation before replacing a selected slot. Settings reset affects preferences only, not campaign state. |\n| Diagnostics and local data | P0 sends no telemetry. Before any consent-gated local diagnostic record is created or exported, state purpose, included minimal fields, retention, deletion control, and that it does not affect play. `Clear diagnostic data` confirms the local-only effect. Never show save contents or dialogue in diagnostics UI. |\n\n## PWA-specific experience\n\nThe web and installed PWA share the campaign, controls, accessibility settings, semantic UI, and local-save behaviour. Installation is an optional convenience: the title/PWA status area explains availability and has a non-modal fallback when the browser offers no install prompt. Offline status is visible but not alarming. An uncached first visit tells the player that the game cannot begin offline until its assets finish downloading; a known-good installed/cache version continues if an update check fails.\n\nAn update notice appears only at a safe boundary (title or paused menu after a confirmed save) and says whether reloading is needed. It offers `Update now` and `Later`; it never reloads during combat, a save write, a modal choice, or essential dialogue. Cache refresh messaging does not imply that save data was refreshed, deleted, or synchronised.\n\n## Content and interaction review checklist\n\nBefore a content or UI change ships, review it against this contract: original provenance; clear next action and empty/error copy; keyboard, touch, mouse, gamepad, and semantic assistive-input operation; visible focus and accurate accessible names; contrast and non-colour cues; 44 CSS-px touch targets; 200% text/reflow; captions and reduced motion; safe save/update/recovery behaviour; destructive confirmation; and consent/minimisation for any privacy-sensitive interface.\n\n## Open decisions retained for approval\n\n- Exact content quantities, party size, localisation languages, full control-remapping depth, audio scope, and supported browser/device/assistive-technology matrix.\n- The legal, age/parental, regional, and certification policies that may change content warnings or accessibility obligations.\n- Account identity, remote sync, multiplayer, player safety/moderation, live operations, commerce, and analytics. These remain outside the offline P0 experience; no social, payment, reporting, account, or remote-data UI may be designed as committed functionality without approval.\n\ncodex\n{\"status\":\"needs_user_review\",\"summary\":\"No files were changed. B0002 is explicitly dependent on B0001 acceptance, but the allocation ledger records B0001 as `blocked_on_gate` pending GATE-0001 / DEC-0001. Creating its R-series contracts now would violate the manifest dependency and checkpoint rules.\",\"changed_files\":[],\"test_result\":\"Read and cross-checked projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json, projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json, projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0001.md, the canonical specification index, and relevant technical/experience contract excerpts. No implementation checks were run because the prerequisite batch is not accepted.\",\"evidence\":{\"agent_log\":\"Manifest evidence: batches.B0001.state is `blocked_on_gate`; B0002.depends_on_batches contains `B0001`; B0002.preceding_decision states `B0001 accepted`; B0001 requires GATE-0001 / DEC-0001.\",